#passage(("There's a verse in the Quran that explains the process of how to deal with disobedient woman. So now we're not talking about a righteous woman. We're talking about an evil woman that, for example, curses her husband, attacks her husband, raises her voice. We're talking about an evil type of woman. How do you deal with them? Allah says, first, you give them advice. For how long? For as long as it works. You advise, you sit down, you talk: \"This is not good. Be good, obey God, fear God. This is not the right relationship.\" As much as you can. If it's not working, then Allah says forsake them in bed, so you can try to get them to feel something in their hearts, so they can [stop] evil behavior and turn away and come back to God.", "Now, if that doesn't work, then there's a last stage here where he uses the word daraba, which is famously quoted by non-Muslims. Now, how is that explained? This is explained by Abdullah ibn Abbas, who was the cousin of Prophet Muhammad, that Prophet Muhammad told us to learn the Quran from him, interpretation of the Quran. Prophet Muhammad made this [supplication] that God would give him understanding the religion. So he said to us, daraba here in the verse refers to a siwak. It's like a pen. Hitting someone with a pen. This is the limit in which you can do in Islam.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Christian Woman Attempts To Throw A Jab At Muslims But Gets Confronted With Her Bible! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GVye2ZP4x1E", ((793, "13:13–13:56"), (836, "13:56–14:29"),))
// U ML335:381-499 | The Muslim Lantern | 4
#passage(("Now this idea, وَاضْرِبُوهُنَّ: in the Arabic language, ضرب does not mean necessarily what you think, which is a severe physical beating. So the word ضرب in The Arabic language can have different levels. So you cannot take it to the extreme without evidence that it can go to the extreme, especially when The Prophetﷺ explicitly told us ضرباً غير مُبَرِّح. What is ضرب مبرح in The Arabic language? Is severe hitting which leaves a mark or can break a bone or do severe damage to that woman. So you're not allowed to do any of that. So it is a form of physical discipline that cannot go to the extreme of leaving a mark, breaking a bone etc., or any of these types of things.", "So even that last option, it's not Allah saying you have to do this; it's an option for some people to do, and Allah has placed restrictions on those men that they cannot go far now and do something extreme. A slap is not acceptable, because The Prophetﷺ said (you cannot hit the face). It's not physical violence.", "Everything I said to you right now is in the Tafaseer (exegeses). Tafsir AtTabary, who died in 310AH, over a 1100 or 1200 ago, he's saying the same thing I'm saying. So this got nothing to do with my belief. And that time there was no Feminism. They were clearly explicitly saying what the teachings are. They're not lying about anything. So you open Tafsir AtTabary, you see exactly what I said. Now the one who said there is restrictions on ضرب is not me, it's The Prophetﷺ. He said ضرباً غير مُبَرِّح; that is not me, that's a Hadith. So it's not me adding an explanation; it's The Prophetﷺ explicitly telling what you're allowed to do and what you're not allowed to do.", "And there comes a point in which, yes, a man has to use a level of physical discipline with his wife. What if your wife starts hitting you? What if your wife starts becoming abusive herself? What if your wife starts cursing you and disrespecting you in every way, shape or form? I'm saying in the end, if nothing is working with the women, for you to apply a form of physical discipline, to establish that you are actually being serious about this, with the conditions that Islam has put, is something that we have no issues with, that we completely believe in as a Muslim. But the point is: what is rationally wrong with everything that we said?",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Tunisian Girl Questions Muslim On Women's Rights! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=l68vK6nDhbA", ((1078, "17:58–19:28"), (1178, "19:38–21:18"), (1293, "21:33–22:15"), (1337, "22:17–23:16"),))
