#passage(("I do have my own copy of [Tafsir al-Tabari] right here; I have access to the Arabic language, which you don't, David. Under verse number 34, [Ibn Abbas is] asked what is meant by a light beating, and he responds: it is a beating with the toothbrush. The same thing that David claims is a whitewashed version of Islam, something that supposedly doesn't exist in the sources, when reality this is not only a first century understanding, this is a first generation understanding. This is the cousin of the prophet, peace be upon him, saying that this is what it is: that this is about beating with a miswak. It doesn't mean beat her senselessly.",), "Farid Responds, “You will lose faith in David Wood after watching this video. (Wife-beating)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nVjMAr1oO7Q", ((143, "2:23–3:22"),))
// U FR374:213-245 | Farid Responds | 5
#passage(("As for premise one, we can simply say that lack of total equality between sexes is not an implication of oppression, as oppression by definition is the exercise of authority or power in a burdensome, cruel or unjust manner. The Islamic view of limiting a woman's movement, for example by three days of travel on her own, or the prohibition on her being a [caliph], or discouragement from attending the military, is all done for the sake of uplifting burdens. These are examples of these inequalities. When it comes to travel, for example, it is done in order to protect her from harm, as it is seen that generally women have a more difficult time defending themselves in unknown environments. A proof of this would be the fact that 90% or more of rape which occurs in such environments is done by men.", "As for the prohibition of being a [caliph], its benefits include women avoiding the responsibility of political turmoil while being pregnant or needing to take care of the children or going through menopause cycles. Generally, the Islamic goal for society, other than establishment of religion, is establishment of healthy families, and that would necessitate widespread marriage. So this should not be dismissed easily. As for attending the military, it is so because the [prophet] forbade the killing of women and children in war. So it is only logical that he would not want them to be exposed to danger, let alone dying.", "All rulings of Islam, other than being justified by the fact that they are orders given by God, also have their [maqasid], pre-intended purposes that are found there for just reasons and the purpose of uplifting burdens, that which is opposite to oppression. So the idea of total and absolute equality being a necessity for not oppressing women falls off, as it is not necessary for avoiding oppression.", "In regards to hitting one's wife, this, along with the divine command theory approach, is justified by simply saying that conditions are put upon the man before he is allowed to strike his wife lightly, as approved by the [prophet] according to many hadiths and [Ibn Kathir] and the similar. These conditions include the man first consulting his wife, and then after a period of time, if that does not solve the issue in the family, separation from bed is permitted, and if that seems to be futile, then the last resort is hitting one's wife lightly. All of these three steps are only permitted in the case of a woman showing arrogance, which in the Arabic is called [nushuz], which includes the following: acting as if she's above her husband, disobeying him, ignoring him, disliking him, and so forth.", "There is a difference of opinion as to what the husband's right actually is. For example, one of these narrations only mentions that the husband's right is that he can have the right to prohibit anyone he wants from being a guest in his house. That's it. Now, obviously, this is not the only right that the husband has over his wife. And similarly wives have many rights over their husbands. But the point is that the purpose of these rights is so that people, both men and women, husbands and wives, avoid mental and physical abuse. So the permission for hitting one's wife is done for the sake of uplifting burdens, which is again opposite to oppression. This means that both of the key factors of [Ridvan's] premises fall apart, and therefore it follows that the conclusion is also false or not true.",), "Farid Responds, “[DEBUNKED] Islam vs Women - Apostate Prophet”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Fu7m4EFyuJc", ((1170, "19:30–20:20"), (1220, "20:20–20:53"), (1257, "20:57–21:22"), (1282, "21:22–22:14"), (1339, "22:19–23:09"),))

