#passage(("If the daughter is putting work into the company, does she have a share or not? If she has a share, then she takes her share. That's not a part of the inheritance. I'm saying if the daughter has a share of the company, like she has 20%, 30%, because she's putting sweat and blood, then the father will give her a percentage. If she has a percentage of the company, it's her right, it's her property. What you have as inheritance is only the father's property.", "Inheritance will be divided in an Islamic portion, so depending on how many children he has, depending on his wife, depending on his parents. One son, one daughter: the son will get two times of the daughter. If there is a wife, she takes ⅛, and the rest will go to the relatives if there are any.", "To you, that is unfair, because what you've done is that you've taken a scenario that you have made up with your own self, in your own mind, and then you remove every other variable in Islam. You have to take the whole picture or you don't take the picture. So, if you want to say Islam is unjust in a specific thing, then you look at how Islam holistically deals with the male and female, and you don't only look at just the case of inheritance. The money she will take from inheritance is her property, while the man has a higher financial obligation in Islam. He has to provide for the wife, he has to pay the Mahr, he has to pay all of these things.", "Your example is flawed. If, for 20 years, she was sweating her blood, he did not give her a part of the company, then you should blame the father, not Islam! You cannot have your own cake and then eat it. You cannot say she was working for 20 years, but all of a sudden, he died! It just doesn't work. She was working for 20 years, which means you had all of this time to give her a piece of the company, which would be the thing that makes sense to do. If she started working the first day and then he died, that makes sense. But for 20 years she put her sweat and blood into it; if the father does not give her her right, then now the blame is on the father. You cannot blame the Islam inheritance system for that.", "You have to look at Islam, where Islam says to the father that: (every father is a shepherd and he will be asked of his flock), which means he has to deal justly with his children, and he will be held accountable on the Day of Judgment for that, if he denies something of his daughter that is a part of her right. If you're asking me, does X, Y and Z make sense in Islam, you have to look at what the Islamic paradigm says about the issue. You cannot take a part of the Islamic paradigm and then apply your methodology there. This inheritance thing is also under the Day of Judgment, the Hellfire, etc. So, your issue now is not really with the concept of inheritance. If you want to deny the Afterlife, then your issue should be whether Islam is from God or not. That's a separate discussion. If you want to talk about inheritance, you have to take the Islamic point of view, which has the Afterlife as a part of it.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Agnostic Questions The Quran! Muhammed Ali - Speakers Corner”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=P_3UFyI5Xm0", ((54, "0:54–1:14"), (117, "1:57–2:16"), (168, "2:48–3:45"), (253, "4:13–5:07"), (352, "5:52–7:00"),))
// U ML013:155-180 | The Muslim Lantern | 3
#passage(("For instance, they will talk about inheritance: the man gets two times the woman in the inheritance. In the reality, this is utterly false. There's four cases where inheritance is distributed in Islam. One case is where a man would take two times the woman, and this is in cases, for example, where you have a daughter and a son. However, there are cases where they take the same. For example, there are cases where the mother and the father, both men and women, they will take sixth of the inheritance. So, they take equally.", "There is now another type of cases where women will inherit more than men. So, for example, if you have only a daughter, a daughter takes half of the inheritance. Two daughters, they take two-thirds of the inheritance. Father is there, he doesn't take anywhere near that. So, it's not about men or women. There's even cases where women will inherit, and if it was a man in that same case, he wouldn't inherit. So, how is the religion made for the desire of the man? Prohibiting men in inheritance in some cases and giving it to the woman, and having more cases where women are getting more than the men.", "If a woman dies and leaves behind a husband, a father, and a mother, a daughter, and a daughter of a son: if it's a daughter of a son, she will take sixth of the inheritance. But if it's a son of a son, he will take nothing. So, it's the same exact scenario, but it's a man, and the man would not take anything, and the daughter would take something. So, this idea that in inheritance Islam is just giving more to the man is utterly false.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Is Islam A Religion Made For The Men?”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OzxyEO708yI", ((555, "9:15–9:52"), (595, "9:55–10:24"), (624, "10:24–10:47"),))
