#passage(("What is the ruling behind women engaging in [dawah] to begin with? I do not mean sisters doing [dawah] to other sisters. I do not mean sisters on WhatsApp group or sisters in telegram. We need all of that. Or sisters writing books that we can all benefit from, or doing research. All of that is great. Alhamdulillah. I am the biggest proponent of that. I'm talking about sisters doing this idea of where they are in the forefront of the dawa, engaging with men and doing [dawah] here and there, and going on channels and making YouTube videos and lives. What is the ruling behind that? It is not known that any female Sahaba would go out in the midst of non-maharim of other Sahaba and would start giving them dawa and lecturing them. If I'm wrong, you're welcome to show me examples of Sahabia women that would go out in the midst of other Sahaba men that are [non-mahram], and they will stand on a podium or whatever, and they will start giving them lectures and reminders.", "Some people will say, \"she was asked.\" It is correct. Who was she? The mother of the believers, or the other wives; they were the mother of the believers. So it's like your own mother, and they will call her \"our mother\" when they would talk to her. And who were those people going asking? Were the sahaba, the people with the purest hearts and the best of behaviors. I'm not talking about non-Muslims and random Josh mo whatever going; they were the best of sahaba. Because they know many things in the household of prophet Muhammad, the other people didn't know, so they would go and ask.", "Now we're saying: she was their mother; they were the best of mannerisms and hearts. But not just that: Allah tells them in the Quran, if you ask them for something, ask them behind the barrier. These are people with the best of hearts. They were the mothers of the believers. But Allah is still saying ask them behind the barrier. Allah says that is more pure for your hearts and their hearts. Who are we talking about? The wives of the [prophet], the best women, and the [sahaba]. And of course the wives of the prophet are the examples for the Muslim sisters. Just like the sahaba are examples and the prophets are example, the wives of the prophet are examples for the women. Allah tells [them] in the Quran: \"and remain in your houses\", meaning in most cases; if you need something, to go out, if there's a need. This is what [Allah] commanded the wives of the prophet. Dawa is a job for men to do.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “This Trend NEEDS To Stop Now!”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Mc-2HiePa94", ((1047, "17:27–18:22"), (1102, "18:22–19:21"), (1161, "19:21–20:20"),))
