#passage(("There is no such thing in the Quran or Hadith where it says that women cannot seek education. Now, there is a completely different idea, which is the idea of priorities. What should be the priority for a woman? That's a completely different story. What is beneficial for a society as a whole? Is it beneficial for society, as a whole, that a woman seeks higher education that, depending on the profession, will take what we would say: the most beneficial, important years of her life? From a masculine or a male perspective, the years that she's most beautiful, most fertile. That is just a fact. It's just a reality, a biological fact.", "So is it a priority to put education first, in which then you would say: I spent X amount of years learning, now I want to work because I don't want this time wasted? Now that goes into both of the parents working. And now you don't have time for one another because they're both tired, stressed, fighting with each other. But most importantly, they don't have time for the child or the children, the household, the raising. So they bring a nanny and this nanny ends up taking the role of the mother. And then she raises a child and in many cases abuses the child. And then that child grows up with complexes because they didn't have their parents when they were growing up. I come across many situations like this. The parents will give them things, they give them financial things, but it does not fill the hole that that child has, as a need for the parents. And then when that child grows up, then they go rogue and they do stuff in their teenage years, rebellion years, because they didn't have that love given to them when they were younger. So all of these things, I'm now talking about a huge ripple effect that takes place on a societal level.", "Now, it's all a question of priority. If women are putting a priority, which is important, and in the same time they're learning, there's no issues. Let's say some woman is learning, she's learning from home or she goes to university or whatever it is, but in the same time, she's able to keep as a priority that which is important, whether that be the children, whether that be the husband, if she's married. And those important years of that woman's life, the most desired years of that woman's life, are not wasted, are not gone; then we are happy with that.", "But nobody can come and say that a woman cannot learn, a woman cannot seek education. That is complete garbage. The Prophetﷺ, he said that \"seeking knowledge is an obligation on every Muslim\", and that includes all types of beneficial knowledge, Islamic knowledge and other knowledge. Allah says in the Quran (Taha20:114) that the believer should say: وَقُلْ رَبِّ زِدْنِي عِلْمًا (O my Lord, increase me in knowledge). And that includes all types of beneficial knowledge.", "There are many fields in which we would require women, for example. These women are not saying that it is good for them that they've been in that situation, but they're still required. You need a woman that is a doctor for your wife. Rather than having a man to check your wife, you want a woman to check your wife. So, of course, it's going to be difficult for that woman that acquires that degree, but they're needed. So you need women in these types of positions, because you want a woman to do that role, instead of a man doing it. So, it's not a black and white answer, don't seek education or seek education. I think you look at the case by case situation, and then you see what is the priority that should be for that specific person or individual. And based on that, the girl should pursue that priority.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Agnostic Questions Muslim On The Education Of Women In Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K1ADYkUV9-Q", ((224, "3:44–4:17"), (261, "4:21–5:24"), (332, "5:32–6:03"), (367, "6:07–6:27"), (390, "6:30–7:12"),))
