#passage(("Islam is a rational religion as well. This is how marriages work: it's with agreement. A woman might find a good husband that she cannot find somewhere else. And he will say: not necessarily I'm going to marry another wife, but if you want to, if you love me and you want to be with me, or if you see me as someone that you're not going to find someone else like, these are my conditions. And a woman might say: okay, I'm beautiful, I'm from this family, I'm rich, whatever it is, these are my conditions. Marriage is about agreement. It's about two people that are compatible with one another. So that marriage contract is about people agreeing to terms and agreements with one another. And if they agree, you get married. If they don't, you can always find someone else. It's how things are.", "So in Islam, there's a marriage contract. The woman is allowed to put a condition that: okay, I don't want my husband to marry more than one wife. But that doesn't mean he cannot marry more than one wife. He can still do that in the future, because that's something that God allowed him to do. But if he does, then you're allowed to break the marriage, and you keep your marital gift. When he marries you, something called [mahr]: used to give you money, gold, this, that.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Christian Lady Questions Muslim On Polygamy! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=128SY3Jfgds", ((254, "4:14–5:19"), (319, "5:19–5:49"),))
