#passage(("They say 50% divorce rate, and 70 to 80% initiated by women, 90% initiated by educated women. It's talking about the US.", "Over 95% of Muslims, or even more, are not married to more than one wife. So why do you have to be one of the ones? You don't have to be! So in Islam, in our tradition, in the jurisprudence of the Hanbali Madhhab, what you can do: you can stipulate a condition before the marriage: the husband cannot marry another wife. You cannot change the marriage contract; it has to remain the same. Whatever agreed upon in the marriage contract, you cannot come later on and say I change it.", "Also, submit there is not like worship: when you say it to the woman \"do this\", she is gonna do it! I think there's a bit of a wrong perception here about the marriage. Submission is more when there is a disagreement. You yourself mentioned the reason why the divorce is there. You say I don't want to have divorce, but I'm going to do what the people in America are doing, feminism is pushing them to do, which is to be equal to a man and argue with a man and not listening to the man.", "So what's the alternative? There's only one alternative: the woman listens to the husband in that which is good only, in that which is good, in that which is righteous. الطاعة في المعروف: the Prophetﷺ said (the obedience is that in which is good). He cannot command her to do something bad. A marriage is a unity in Islam. It's where everyone understands his role: a woman understands her role, and a man understands his role. And both they understand they have different roles to play. It's not: there is this oppressive leader and there's this subordinate that just listens to whatever the oppressive leader... These marriages cannot work. They don't work, and that's not the teachings of Islam.", "Our example is Prophet Muhammadﷺ. How was he in his marriage? For example, one of his wives, she got jealous, so she was sitting with other people and she smashed the plates on the floor. How did the Prophetﷺ behaved? This idea of oppressive leadership, you'll say: How? And this! How can you do! No! All he said: your mother became jealous. Even though a lot of people can say: oh look, this is disrespecting her husband by doing this and that. A lot of people can perceive it that way. There was instances where Aisha was literally screaming at the Prophetﷺ, and then her father was there, so he's the one who pushed her away from the Prophetﷺ. Can you imagine? He's not just a man, he is the best of men, and he's the Prophet of Allah, and she was screaming at him!", "This perception that certain people have, this is a red pill kind of an idea that is not Islam. Islam doesn't say: I got this concubines, I just say to them go left, they go left, go right, they go right. That is not a teaching of Islam. Islam is balanced. Islam says the man is the leader in the relationship, and women want a leader in the end. So they want a leader, but leader in reasonable proportions, and telling them to do that which is good, not in an oppressive manner.", "And as I said, there are many women who are happy being second wives, and there are second wives today. And there are women who can say: OK, well, you know what, I want to put this condition in my contract: you do not marry another one. Most men, they don't have the capacity to do it anyways. Not just that, you have to be physically able to do it, you're financially able to do it, you have to be able to handle the pressure from more than one woman, and you have to be just with all of them in that which you can be just in. Like if you buy the financial things, or anything that you can be just, and you buy her a car, you buy her a car. Things you can be just in, then you have to be just in. It's not easy. I know many people who are rich, they say I want to get married to another wife, and I just tell them the conditions; they say: No no! Forget it, I changed my mind, one is enough.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Christian Lady Enquires About Heaven In Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NyrpGRsef5I", ((2528, "42:08"), (2556, "42:36–43:01"), (2584, "43:04–43:38"), (2623, "43:43–44:21"), (2664, "44:24–44:57"), (2702, "45:02–45:25"), (2731, "45:31–46:24"),))

