#passage(("The second point you mentioned was about the Prophetﷺ marrying multiple wives. Do you know how many wives Solomon had? He had 1,000 (1kings11:3). David had a hundred (2Samuel5:13). Abraham had multiple wives, three I believe or more (Genesis20:2, 16:3, 25:1). Moses had multiple wives (Exodus2:21), (Numbers12:1). All of these main Prophets of God had multiple wives. You said it doesn't mean it was OK. That's incorrect, because there isn't a single verse where God condemns them for that. There are verses in the Old Testament that talks about how to deal with women: if you have more than one wife, how to deal with them (Exodus21:10). So that means that God is OK with this idea. There's only a talk about Solomon, because of an action he did, he was punished for (1Kings11:9-13), or talk about David, because of an action he did, he was punished for (2Samuel12:10-14). There's not any verses that condemn having more than one wife. None!", "The Prophet said: (the best of you are the best to their wives). When he says 'wives', means that there are multiple believers, so it's wives in plural. That doesn't mean that believers have many wives. It just means that because there are many believers, you would say 'wives'. They're not one believer. So he says \"the best of you are the best to their wives\", because he's talking to a multitude of people.", "Who said that there was incest anyways? That's a position that the biblical narrative says. Islam doesn't say anything about that. How they multiplied in that time, Allah knows best. Allah didn't tell us. So, we're not taking that position. In the beginning of the Creation, of course, there's two people in the beginning, but you're leaving every other case of every other Prophet of God. And these were righteous people, the best of creation: Moses, Abraham, Adam, all of these people. They're better than any human being that exists, and God allowed them to do that.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Christian Wife Grills A Muslim With Difficult Questions! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tUX4xfgST_E", ((468, "7:48–9:11"), (567, "9:27–9:43"), (601, "10:01–10:30"),))
