#passage(("What is a child? We use terms in conversation, and we need to clarify terms in order for us to reach a conclusion. You cannot just ask me what is Islam, and I tell Islam is Islam. Of course there has to be a definition of the thing that we're talking about. For example, if I call you a child, you might be insulted, because you're not a child. So if you're not a child, that means there is a definition of what a child is, and that definition doesn't fit you.", "If you talk about full brain development, it happens to women at the age of 25 to be fully completed. That means you're saying every girl under 25 is a child! Of course, that makes no sense. Women that are under 25 are not children, they're still adults. So it's not about brain development. In every dictionary in human history, a child was someone who was under the age of puberty. If you go to any dictionary 40 or 50 years ago, throughout all human history, that was the definition of a child: someone who is under the age of puberty. Medical experts don't tell you that you need full brain development to become an adult. Nobody says that.", "Why do I need to accept that magically, if a person is 17 years old, 364 days, 23 hours and 59 minutes, they're a child, but after one minute they become an adult, magically? I don't accept that: this idea of you becoming an adult when you hit the age of 18, just because the West told you you become an adult when you hit the age of 18. Not all the West, by the way. The overwhelming majority of the world right now, it doesn't say the age of consent is 18. The overwhelming majority of the world right now is 16. You look at the USA even, you have states that has it as 16, 15, 14, with judge and parental agreement. There are child marriages happening in California every year, hundreds of thousands. You can just research it right now: in California, in the US, we're not even talking about Muslim countries.", "Common sense is to look at case by case scenarios. Common sense is to look at all human history. We don't apply fallacies of presentism. Presentism is to apply your current day, modern standards, on the past. The past was different, it had different things; we don't apply things in the past the way we do today. Now, when we look historically, an adult was always someone who is over the age of puberty; that was always the case. We're not going to change it today, just because some people feel a certain way. Feelings don't change realities. The reality was and always is: a child is someone who is under the age of puberty.", "When we stay on the surface, on an ignorant surface level, this is where people get confused. When we get into the nitty-gritty and details, and realise the realities of things, then we realise: OK, I thought there was an issue here, but the issue is in my understanding, not that in reality there is a problem.", "If we agree that a child is someone who is under the age of puberty, do women hit puberty before the age of 13? Then to say the statement that 13 years old equals a child is not correct, because women can reach puberty at even the age of 8, from 8 to 14. By the way, who says that? The NHS, the National Health Service in the UK. It's not my stats. They can be as early as 8 years old, which is called early puberty, and it can be as late as 14.", "The Islamic contract of marriage, the عقد النكاح in Arabic, the marriage contract, that marriage contract doesn't need an age; you can do it with a baby, no problem. But there is a difference between doing a marriage contract, and having intercourse. There are many people who do a marriage contract with someone who's abroad. Doing a marriage contract does not necessitate physical intercourse. So we have to separate these things. We don't misunderstand the idea of marriage in the West and apply it to Islam. What does that mean? It means I can do عقد النكاح with someone without دخول, without consummation of the marriage.", "Why would you want to do that? This is what Islamic scholars have talked about for hundreds of years. They said, for example, let's say a father sees a very great person, someone who he knows if he leaves, someone else will take that person, then he'll get married to someone else. He wants to secure that person in a way, so he would ask him to do a marriage contract with his daughter, as the father, until his daughter reaches the age of puberty, and then be able to consummate the marriage. Another example, let's say a father is very poor, he cannot take financial responsibility of his daughter. His daughter is still not ready yet for consummation, so he finds a man, and he gives him this marriage contract, so he can take care, financially, of her. And then he will wait until she reaches the age of puberty, and then the consummation will take place, but the father now will not have the financial burden of taking care of the daughter, because he's poor. There's many situations like this that scholars have mentioned. But in the end, it doesn't change the reality: consummation and marriage contract are not the same in Islam.", "There are conditions that scholars have put forward, that Shawkani mentions, Imam Shafi’i mentions, others mention, for the father doing a marriage contract for his daughter if she's under the age of puberty, or not ready for consummation. First, only the father, not the grandfather, not the brother, not the uncle, nobody else, only the father can do this. Number two, there has to be a benefit for the kid. This contract that is going to be done has to be in the benefit of the child long-term. It cannot be done for other reasons. The father doesn't have the capacity to do it just because he likes to do it. Third thing is, Imam Shafi'i mentions, when she reaches the age of puberty or maturity, she's asked if she's OK or not with the marriage. Why? Based on the Hadith of the Prophetﷺ. He said: لَا تُنْكَحُ الْبِكْرُ حَتَّى تُسْتَأْذَنَ (The virgin or the young girl is not given in marriage until [her] permission is asked). So Imam Shafi'i said, when she reaches the age of maturity, puberty, then she's asked, based on this Hadith, is she OK, or that marriage is not going to continue, because her agreement is necessary in this case. And there are other conditions.", "After the age of puberty, we consider someone an adult in Islam; he's no longer a child. And that's why in Islam you're accountable for your deeds, the man or woman, after you reach the age of puberty. If you reach the age of puberty, now you are considered an adult Islamically. Now your deeds are being recorded, man or woman, there's no difference here.", "Which father is going to want harm for his own daughter? Someone might say the father is evil. OK, some evil father: that evil father, we said, if he does this marriage, it has to be in the benefit of the girl. If he doesn't do it in the benefit of the girl, the judge can break this marriage, and he can be punished. The father is not the one who is going to get married, but the father is the experienced parent, who literally gave birth to this person, who would want good for them. Just like you look at your own kids, that father will look the same way.", "You cannot use the word \"common sense\" when you just talk about yourself, because common sense is applicable to people generally. If I bring 100 people and ask them something, 99 of them would agree, at least, if not 100. That's what common sense is. Everything I've presented so far is not my opinion. I presented to you the opinions of people who are experts.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Arab Apostate Confronts Muslim On The Age Of Marriage In Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CXLU_RiHFrk", ((90, "1:30–2:54"), (207, "3:27–6:24"), (504, "8:24–9:16"), (671, "11:11–11:42"), (729, "12:09–12:22"), (796, "13:16–13:51"), (892, "14:52–15:28"), (938, "15:38–16:56"), (1054, "17:34–18:42"), (1135, "18:55–19:09"), (1159, "19:19–20:12"), (1292, "21:32–21:52"),))
