#passage(("You need to understand Ibn [Kathir's] position before you talk about Ibn [Kathir's] position. Because what people are doing is they take everything in a vacuum. They don't take the holistic opinion of Ibn [Kathir]. So you need to collect all Ibn [Kathir's] ideas about that marriage first. When these scholars speak, they speak with certain assumptions that exist in Islamic texts. For instance, we have something called \"the harm principle\", which is one of the main five principles in jurisprudence in Islam. And that principle is from the Hadith of the Prophet. He says: لا ضَرَرَ ولا ضِرارَ (You're not allowed to cause harm to anyone else or yourself). You don't cause harm to yourself, you don't cause harm to anyone else. So if any marriage with any woman at any age, if you're going to cause her harm, physical or psychological, and that can be established, then Ibn [Kathir], and every scholar out there, is going to say to you that is Haram.", "Generally in Islam we have someone who's called الوَلي. A waliy will be the guardian of the girl. It doesn't necessarily have to be the father; if the father is not there, it can be someone else. But if the girl is young, the scholars say, it HAS TO BE the father, specifically the father being the guardian.", "The scholars would mention these exceptional cases and scenarios. None of these are recommendations for Muslims to go do. That's not: go and marry this, or do specifically that. So it has to be the father, it has to be for the benefit of the girl, there can be no harm, physical or psychological, on the girl. And then if these conditions are all applied and valid, and there is no issues, we don't have an issue with it. Where is the immorality there?",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Christian Questions Muslim On Young Marriages Then Instantly Regrets It! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zHZPZujQbd8", ((439, "7:19–8:09"), (508, "8:28–8:35"), (573, "9:33–10:11"),))
