#passage(("I'm not going to say that it's a phenomena of kids leaving Islam. That's not the phenomena. The phenomena is that the parenting is not being done the right way, and that is what is leading these kids to leave, because Islam is a truth. We know Islam is the biggest growing religion in the world. We know everyone has evidences for Islam. An overwhelming majority of these kids end up becoming non-religious or joining some crazy movement, like I said, rainbow flags or whatever. It's not about Islam not being the truth. It is about the parenting that is being done to these kids in most cases. Of course, I'm not saying that if your kid leaves Islam, that means you're a bad parent necessarily. No. It could happen where you do everything in your power and Allah tests you. It can happen. However, in most cases, the case is that the parenting is the bad parenting, and not that the kid is the one who ended up becoming a different religion or following a different path.", "When you're teaching your kid, you're just telling them: do this, without giving them any rationality, without giving them any explanation. And a lot of the parents end up using strict methods, being very harsh with the kids. Some of them can beat them. Oh, don't question, don't ask. Just believe. A lot of the parents are applying this method. And it is sad. You tell your kid: \"Do this, do that. It's time to pray. Wake up, pray. Do this, do that.\" He doesn't understand. What's the reason? What's the purpose? Why am I doing this? Why do we believe in this? I'm doing this because my parent tells me to do this. I'm not doing this because I understand the reason behind it. And I understand many of us have grew up, many Muslims have grew up, in families where they had the same treatment. They had the parents telling them, \"Just do this. We're born Muslims, we're born this way, so just go pray. Go do this.\" We need to understand ourselves before we talk about our kids. We need to understand ourselves