#passage(("Sometimes they come to me: \"Do you have a book that teaches a kid Islam?\" He thinks his kid, 8 years old, 9 years old, 10 years old, is going to give him a booklet and the kid is going to become Sheikh al-Islam Ibn Taymiyyah tomorrow. Or: \"Oh, do you have a website? Do you have this?\" What kind of joke is this? What kind of a parent is this? If you're not going to spend the time and sit down with your kid teaching them yourself, you think you're going to give them a booklet like school does and you think he's going to learn, he's going to understand, he's going to be convinced? You think you're talking to someone who's 40 years old now, or what? You have to build the foundation in your kids.", "This is not some sort of a joke. This is a serious issue. And some people don't understand the seriousness of this issue. The Prophet said, \"There is none among you who is responsible for another person or the affairs of another subject.\" Like the parents, because the Prophet said, \"All of you are shepherds and responsible for your flock.\" So, Prophet says there is none among you who is like this, who's a shepherd responsible for his flock at any level (you could be a ruler or you could be a parent), and then he says, \"and he died in a state in which he was dishonest in fulfilling that role, in taking care of the subject, except that paradise is going to be forbidden from him.\" Can you imagine? This hadith is in Sahih Muslim.", "You're going to have a kid. That kid, just like he's going to be one of the three things that would benefit you after you die, like the Prophet said, \"If the child of Adam dies, his deeds will stop except from three things.\" One of them is a righteous kid that makes dua for you. How is he going to be a righteous kid when you're not even taking care of them? Just like that kid can be that type of benefit, he's also a trial for you. Allah says in the Quran, \"Verily your wealth and your children are a test for you.\" So, that kid is a test for you. If you don't take care of that kid, it can be your damnation in the afterlife, cuz it's our responsibility. It's not just like you gave birth to someone and you let them go and you think that they're going to learn everything on their own.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “If You're Doing This, There's A Chance Your Kid Leaves Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r632sKczYqY", ((627, "10:27–11:01"), (662, "11:02–11:41"), (708, "11:48–12:25"),))
// U ML022:259-306 | The Muslim Lantern | 4
#passage(("Something very important now to talk about, which is the idea of homeschooling. Whoever is in the West and has the ability to home school their kids, they need to home school their kids. Your kid not getting an education is in most cases would be better than taking your kids to a Catholic school where they're going to teach them rainbow flag values and they're going to teach them disbelief values and you're not even taking care of them. Them being with no education home is in many cases better than you doing that to them, because their afterlife is more important than this life.", "You're not a good parent because your kid has a degree. You're a good parent because you taught your kid the right values and the right morals, which he will grow up with and he will carry on as a torch for his own kids and benefiting his community. This is what a good parent is. A lot of the parents have this stupid idea, or they think they're good kids because their kid is an engineer. They send their daughters to become engineers and doctors rather than raising the value of being a wife in their daughter and getting her married in her early days, she's in her early 20s, getting her married. No, they're making her spend 10 years in some random school or doing this or doing that. Of course, we need some female doctors to take care in exceptional cases where a woman needs a doctor. However, what is a priority for you to teach your kid? We're not teaching our kids to be men and women anymore. We're not teaching them the right values to begin with.", "So, homeschooling is a very important thing. If you are in the West, it's either you take your kids to a Muslim school, which is a good Muslim school. Not all Muslim schools are good. Some Muslim schools are just like Muslim schools, but they're not teaching proper mannerisms to the kids. Even if you send them to a Muslim school that's not great, it's better. Either a Muslim school or you home school your kids. This is a responsibility upon you. I've met some Muslims who took their kids to Catholic schools. I couldn't believe it, but they do it. You don't take your kid to a non-Muslim school. Don't do it, unless you want your kids to leave Islam.", "There's this YouTuber. I think he accepted Islam. He has a video where he talks about his story. He wasn't even a Muslim, but he believes in God, and how his teacher was mocking him and making fun of him to make him stop believing God, mocking the idea of believing God. And his teacher is an adult, so he knows how to argue. And he's mocking the kid until the kid stops believing in God. This is what happens in many of these schools. Go ask the Muslim teachers what kind of rules they give them to teach the kids. So, unless you really want to risk the afterlife of your kids, try to home school your kids. It's a responsibility upon you.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “If You're Doing This, There's A Chance Your Kid Leaves Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r632sKczYqY", ((827, "13:47–14:17"), (857, "14:17–15:14"), (916, "15:16–16:02"), (969, "16:09–16:48"),))
// U ML284:1305-1377 | The Muslim Lantern | 4
#passage(("If you live in the West and you think, because you are عبدُ الدرهم like the Prophetﷺ said, you worship the درهم (Dirham), the money, you think that taking your children to school and ending up being Atheist and Agnostic in a mixed school, where they teach them Rainbow values, Colourful values, is more important than their آخرة (Afterlife), then you should not have children! I'm telling you, don't have children. You're only causing us more trouble; we would have to deal with them. Stay without children, it's better. You're not helping. The Prophetﷺ, when he said: تَناكَحوا تَكثُرُوا؛ فإنِّي أُباهي بِكمُ الأُمَمَ يَومَ القِيامة (Multiply because he will show us off on the Day of Judgment), those children of yours are not going to be a part of them, so there's no point of multiplying if they're going to be with the other side. If you are going to have children, take the responsibility of your children.", "The school is not going to raise your kids. I have a friend. He has his children in the West and then he wants to travel to a Muslim country. He said, \"I'm going to send them School there, they are going to learn.\" I said to him: Do you think the school is going to raise your children? Muslim country or non-Muslim country, you think the school is going to raise your children? I know the biggest schools, like for example alAzhar in Egypt, one of the biggest Islamic schools: children there, they watch pornography on their phones, and they say the most vicious cursing words. Of course they teach them good, I'm not saying the whole institution is bad, we're saying these things are present. So, to assume that if I take my child to alAzhar, or a good University, he's going to learn, then you are dreaming!", "It's your responsibility to teach your son. You will be asked. You have to have classes in the home, at least one day a week: story about Companions, teaching them their 'Aqeeda. Where is Allah? Allah is above his عرش (Throne), above. Teaching them these things. Belief in the Prophetﷺ. What is the Shahada? What is Iman? Teaching them these things. Alhamdulillah, and there are people who do this. I have a friend's 7 year old daughter. She wants to finish the Quran, memorises a lot of the Quran. She's one of the smartest kids, اللهم بارك (May Allah Bless). So, we can do it, but we need to put in the effort. So either you take them to an Islamic school, because there are Islamic schools; if you can not afford it, you try to homeschool your kids.", "A lot of people come to me, or they come to دُعاة, they come to Scholars, or they come to 'Ulama, or they come to Students of Knowledge, saying: \"Look, my son left Islam.\" No! You made your son leave Islam. You didn't teach your son from when he was a baby, and at the end of the problem, 16, 17, 18 years later, you want me to fix it! Do you know Cancer? Cancer can be treated in the early stages; there's many treatments for Cancer in the early stages. But once cancer seeps through the rest of the body, you can not deal with it anymore. So you don't leave your child for 16, 18 years, has been absorbing these things, and then come later on when you have a problem: \"Oh, fix the problem. Go!\" I'm going to give him a magic pill, and he's going to become Sheikh alislam! It's not going to happen. TAKE CARE of your children. Raise your children upon correct beliefs and Foundations, seek knowledge, have good friends and Companions, and InshaAllah, Allahﷻ will give you guidance. Look at the Companions of the Prophetﷺ, what they did, imitate it, and do likewise.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “The Situation Of The Ummah, The Disease & The Cure | Al Muwahiddeen Mosque - The Netherlands |”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0ycNKKOx2jg", ((4191, "1:09:51–1:10:40"), (4240, "1:10:40–1:11:19"), (4290, "1:11:30–1:12:28"), (4360, "1:12:40–1:13:36"),))
// U ML227:278-306 | The Muslim Lantern | 2
#passage(("I'll tell you why you are at an advantage. Because you don't need to remain in the US, number one, or you don't need to remain where you are in the US now. There are many places in the US which are full of Muslim populations; they have majority Muslim places, Muslim schools, they have Muslim communities. Many places like this in the US: if you go to Jersey, if you go to different places, there are places like this which have a bigger population of Muslims. Or there are other countries, if you're able to relocate. But the reason I'm saying you are at an advantage is because your children are still very young, so you have a long time to do that transition, in which you can put them in that environment at a young age, in an Islamic environment in which, then, it becomes the norm.", "Because, yes, raising children in the West is difficult. The reason is because the surroundings have more of an influence on the child than the household, because they would spend most of the time in the surroundings. So what many Muslims do is, they put the children in homeschooling, because they don't want them to be exposed to rainbows and stuff like that. But that's also a lot of effort, and you need a good wife, a patient wife, etc. It's not an easy thing. You could do it the other way, in which you homeschool and you teach them this and that and you try to balance. But it would be a lot more difficult to do it in a community where the majority is around your big church and all the other children are doing this.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Agnostic Compares Islam And Christianity! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Igzqbqr2Pew", ((1010, "16:50–17:29"), (1049, "17:29–18:32"),))
// U ML022:307-332 | The Muslim Lantern | 1
#passage(("How do we raise our kids the right way? How do we teach our kids? The first thing you need to understand is that you have to be a good example. If you are a mother that doesn't wear the hijab, if you're a father that doesn't pray, doesn't go to the mosque, doesn't listen to Islamic lecture, doesn't do any of these things, and you want your kid to do it, more likely than not, they're not going to do it, cuz they don't see you doing it. They see a hypocrite: someone who's telling me to do something that they themselves are not doing. So, you need to become a good example for the sake of your kids. Fix your own life. Don't be the parent who doesn't pray and tells his kid to go pray. How is he going to pray when you don't pray? Or a woman doesn't wear the hijab and tells her kid, \"Oh, the hijab is nice. You should wear the hijab.\" How is she going to wear the hijab when you don't wear the hijab? Of course, there is exceptions where Allah will guide the kid. But if you're not a good example, more likely than not they're not going to listen to you when you give them advice. That is the first and most important thing that you need to understand.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “If You're Doing This, There's A Chance Your Kid Leaves Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r632sKczYqY", ((1008, "16:48–17:58"),))
// U ML233:1878-2010 | The Muslim Lantern | 5
#passage(("The question is about children: how can you motivate them? How can you lead them by example? They look at you: how do you behave with your family members, how do you behave with your wife, how do you behave with your mother, how do you behave with your children, how do you behave with the people around you. Maybe all of us have had this experience with our own parents: they've told us certain things, and it was an obstacle for us accepting what they were telling us when you were young, because: \"You're telling me to do this, you're telling me to do that, but you're not leading by [example]. I see you doing the opposite.\" The kids will pick up more on what you do than what you say.", "No one is perfect, but there is nothing better than leading by example: if they see you doing good in the community, they see you reciting the Quran often, they see you learning things of knowledge, they see you gathering them. How many of the parents, at least one day a week, and I'm being very generous here, one day a week, you sit down your family and you tell them about a companion? How easy is it for us to do this! 30 minutes, one hour every time, doing quizzes, for example fun quizzes, games where you give them rewards in the end, to do with Islam. Make it fun.", "If you don't lead by example and implement yourself in your household, words only go so far. And that's not only with children, even with wives. Some people marry non Muslim wives, and then in the end what ends up happening is they tell her this, this, this, and he doesn't pray. That's why he ended up marrying one non-muslim woman, because his [deen] is left and right. She's not listening to him, she's not changing anything, because she looks at him: he's doing everything the opposite. Why should I listen? Look at the prophet sallallahu alaihi wasallam: before he tells the people do this or don't do that, he led by [example].", "There's many things that you can introduce, like the things we're mentioning, into the household that can give the love. Because if you don't tell them stories about companions, this and that, they will end up idolizing somebody else. They end up idolizing a streamer, some streamers acting homosexual. So they acting this way, screaming, shouting in the street, acting like a dog, barking. All of these things, they're doing these things today, and they're idealizing them. The kids these days idealize them because there's no alternative. So if there is no alternative, they end up giving someone else the idealization, the love, the support, and they're Muslims; they're supposed to be having a Muslim who is the role model. And that's why, if you ask the child, \"What do you want to be?\" he's going to tell you, \"Want to be a YouTuber. I want to be a [streamer],\" because this is what he's watching, \"I want to be a gamer.\" So if we don't give them an alternative, they're going to end up with this.", "So we need to realize the missing slot and fill it with the lives of [the companions]. If you read some of the biographies and lives, it is enough to make anyone in love with them. Of course the prophet first, that is a necessity; you already tell them the life of the Prophet. But we're saying the companions as well, because there's many different stories, many different characters, many different behaviors that you can give and introduce.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Eddie @TheDeenShowTV Interviews The Muslim Lantern | Masjid Dar Al-Qur'an | Chicago - USA”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sLfJyJGj_eg", ((4125, "1:08:45–1:09:27"), (4175, "1:09:35–1:10:18"), (4221, "1:10:21–1:11:06"), (4269, "1:11:09–1:12:26"), (4346, "1:12:26–1:12:50"),))
// U ML022:333-353 | The Muslim Lantern | 2
#passage(("How many of you, when you were a kid, your father sat you down and said, \"Come here. We're going to learn the foundations of Islam. We're going to learn why we're Muslims. We're going to learn our beliefs. We're going to learn our creed\"? And if the answer is no, that's why we are where we are today.", "Your heart is like a sponge, and this is a description of one of the scholars. If that sponge is full, filled, when you spill something on it, it's not going to absorb it. But if that sponge is empty, it's going to absorb whatever you spill on it, whatever you pour on it. So, if our kids are not being taught anything, they don't have a foundation. We have not instilled any values within our kids: what we believe, why we believe what we believe, why are we Muslims? What is our faith? What is our beliefs? If we haven't instilled these values with them, our children, their heart, everything else will be poured on it, and they will take beliefs from everything around them: their environment, social media, whatever they are.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “If You're Doing This, There's A Chance Your Kid Leaves Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r632sKczYqY", ((1081, "18:01–18:26"), (1111, "18:31–19:18"),))
// U ML221:22-66 | The Muslim Lantern | 1
#passage(("You failed as a parent if you don't teach your children whatever is good for them. We believe all children are born Muslim, which is an [innate] Islamic thing: to believe in a Creator and submit to him. But then the parents would change into Christian, a Jew, a Hindu, whatever it is. The child is born upon that pure State already. You don't make them a Muslim; already born a Muslim. All that you do is you teach them the specific details of Islam and that which is beneficial to them. That's everything that you do, essentially, cuz you know that's good. You don't think that is good, you know that is good. So you want to teach that which is good to your children. Christian will do the same thing: they will think this is good, so they will teach Christianity. And atheist will think, you know what is good: to be open-minded. Keep your child openminded. That's what they believe is good, which is liberalism actually. They believe in liberalism; they don't even know it. So everyone would have an ideology and would believe the ideology is the right ideology, and based on that they will [raise] their children, based on the ideology, whatever it is.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “University Students Surprise Muslim With Difficult Questions! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FTEkpj70bQ0", ((39, "0:39–1:56"),))
// U ML006:1085-1163 | The Muslim Lantern | 3
#passage(("The question is how do you get your kids to act upon their knowledge of Islam. The first and most important thing is to be their example. You are a mother. You put makeup on. You spend the entire day watching TV and movies and Korean pop stars and whatever girls are doing these days. And then you're like to your daughter: \"Oh, read the Quran. Read the Quran. Now, watch the scholar.\" She's going to look at you: \"What are you doing? You're not reading the Quran. Last time I've seen you holding the Quran for 10 minutes was last Ramadan. Why you telling me read the Quran? You don't do it yourself.\" It hurts to hear this, but that's the reality.", "At least twice a week sit your kids down, find some of the stories of the prophets, of the Sahabah, with a good lesson, and then tell them a story. You think kids don't like stories? Even adults like stories. They will look forward to that day where you sit them down and tell them these stories about Islam, and then they will want to imitate that. When you give them a story about how prophet, for example, was being mocked and ridiculed and he stayed upon his faith, you don't think when he goes to school and Mark and Matthew and Luke are mocking him, he remembers that? He remembers that: \"Oh, the prophet, they tortured him and he was upon his religion. I don't care if this idiot is making fun of me.\" But he's not going to have that idea if you haven't taught him this idea when he was younger.", "So if you want your kids to practice the knowledge of Islam, you have to build in them the love of the religion. Faith is never taken as being forceful. I'm talking to you about my experience. Your father is forcing you to pray, to just go to the masjid for the sake of going to the masjid. You just do the movements and you're not really praying. They have to have conviction.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “How Do You Know Which Religion Is From God? A Detailed Comprehensive Analysis | The Muslim Lantern”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9F5gS1yWIRI", ((3904, "1:05:04–1:05:49"), (4025, "1:07:05–1:08:01"), (4084, "1:08:04–1:08:29"),))
// U ML022:354-481 | The Muslim Lantern | 8
#passage(("You have to teach your kids their foundations. We have so many examples in the Quran and the Sunnah and the Hadith. There's this famous narration from Abdullah ibn Abbas where he said one day he was riding behind the Prophet Sallallahu Alaihi Wasallam. And look how the Prophet Sallallahu Alaihi Wasallam is teaching him, and he was a ghulam, he was a young boy. Look how the Prophet Sallallahu Alaihi Wasallam is talking to him. This is how you need to address your kids.", "I still remember till this day: my mother, may Allah be pleased with her and preserve her, she told me something when I was a kid. She was telling me about how to groom and take care of my body and how to trim your hair and stuff. Until this day I remember, because she stood me up and she said, \"Do you know how to do this?\" I said, \"No.\" She said, \"You need to understand: as a Muslim we do this. The Prophet Sallallahu Alaihi Wasallam said this. You need to do this.\" I was a kid, I don't even remember how I looked, but I remember when she told me to do that.", "The Prophet Sallallahu Alaihi Wasallam told him, \"Oh young boy.\" So look, the Prophet Sallallahu Alaihi Wasallam is addressing him. And then he's saying, \"I'm going to teach you a few words.\" We don't even sometimes talk to each other that way as adults. The Prophet is talking to a young boy in this way. He says to him, \"Be watchful of Allah. He will preserve you.\" If you're watchful of Allah and you obey his command, Allah will protect you. He will safeguard you. \"Safeguard his rights. He will be always with you. If you beg, beg only from Allah.\" Look, your kids now, they go, they beg everybody. They would go and beg a random stranger for a piece of candy, because you don't teach them. \"And if you seek assistance, supplicate to Allah alone.\" Look, these values, if you instill in your child, when he is in time of difficulty he's going to remember Allah, he's going to make dua, he's going to call upon Allah. \"And remember that if all the people gather to benefit you, they will not be able to benefit you except what Allah wants for you.\" When the kid understands that: if everybody comes and everybody wants to give me money, everyone wants to help me, if Allah is not happy with me, if Allah is not pleased with me, if Allah is not willing this, I'm not going to get it. And if everybody comes against me and they try to harm me, if everybody's my enemy, I shouldn't be scared, because none of them can do anything to me except for Allah. We have these narrations where the prophet is teaching young kids so we can copy and do the same thing and teach our children. We don't just read these narrations, this hadith, these verses for the sake of reading them.", "Allah tells us in the Quran, in Surah Luqman, Allah gives us an example of how Luqman was teaching and advising his son, so you can bring your son and do the same thing. Allah says in the Quran, \"When Luqman said to his son, advising him\" (what we're saying: bringing your children, sitting them down to give them advice, to teach them), \"Oh my son, do not commit shirk with Allah. Indeed, shirk is a grave sin.\" His young son: he's teaching him, he's giving him these words so he can understand, so he can learn in the future. You instill within your child tawhid, monotheism, not associating with God, not being a polytheist, from a very young age. Then he says to him, \"My dear son, were to weigh a mustard seed and it is hidden inside a rock or in all of the heavens or all of the earth, Allah is able to bring it. Surely, Allah is the most subtle, the all-aware.\" The kid then internalizes, understands that Allah has the ability, the power over all this universe that he's created. He's able to control everything. So you do not despair when you are in a bad situation. You understand that Allah is the creator, that has the power over everything.", "Then he talks to him about the deeds: \"My son, establish the prayer. [Enjoin] the good and forbid the evil. And be patient upon that which happens to you. Surely, this is a resolve to aspire to.\" And then he says to him, \"And do not turn your nose up to the people. Do not [walk] pridefully on the earth. Allah does not like those who are boastful and arrogant.\" You teach your kid not to be like that from a young age, so he can grow up on these characteristics. \"Be moderate in your pace. Lower your voice. For indeed, the ugliest of voices is the braying of a donkey.\"", "So, when you teach your kid from a very young age: this is the characteristics, this is the behavior that you take, this is the foundations that you believe in. We are Muslims because of the following: Islam is the truth. Prophet Muhammad was a messenger from God. He was sent to mankind. Allah chose Prophet Muhammad because of his pure heart. And Allah sent all of these prophets and messengers because Allah loves us, wants to take us out from misguidance into guidance, into light. And he gives us all of these commands cuz these commands are for our benefit. Allah doesn't benefit from it. We are the ones who benefit from it. We do them because they are beneficial for us. And then you give them examples of when someone makes something, they tell you how to interact with it, because they know what's best for it. They created it. Allah created us. He knows what's best for us. Then you teach your kid why you're Muslims, why we believe what we believe. And you make a connection between them and their creator.", "You have to regularly sit your kids down and teach them this. You teach them this belief: to rely on Allah alone. And to teach them, of course, the basics of their practices: that you need to know yourself how to pray, what is the [arkan] of the prayer. You need to teach your kids these things. You need to grow up with the correct foundation, because once [their] sponge is filled, then you don't worry about other influences influencing them, because they know. They grew up knowing that Allah is the only true God. They understand that there is no other gods out there. They understand why they're believers. They understand why they pray to Allah. They understand why Islam is the truth.", "Then you give them role models. Teach your kid the stories of the Sahaba. Teach your kid the stories of the prophets. And when you teach them, you teach them a value in the story. You teach them the courageous nature of Ali ibn Abi Talib in his battles. You teach them the wisdom of Abu Bakr as-Siddiq. You teach them the strength of Umar ibn al-Khattab. And you bring them a story. There is many examples that we have. And this has to be regular. Then a kid starts to look up to someone. And then when you teach them the value, when that same situation happens to them, they remember: \"I have to be honorable, because Ali ibn Abi Talib was honorable, because Uthman ibn Affan was honorable. I should be honorable in this situation. This Sahabi forgave the other Sahabi when he did this to him; I should forgive my friend when my friend does something bad to me or curses me or insults me.\" These values are only going to come from these stories and these role models that you build in your child's life. Once your child has a role model they look up to, they will want to imitate. They will want to do the same thing they did. They will want to learn more about them. That is not going to come on its own. You have to do that. You have to put that effort as a parent.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “If You're Doing This, There's A Chance Your Kid Leaves Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r632sKczYqY", ((1159, "19:19–19:40"), (1182, "19:42–20:13"), (1215, "20:15–21:33"), (1295, "21:35–22:59"), (1379, "22:59–23:33"), (1413, "23:33–24:26"), (1466, "24:26–25:05"), (1507, "25:07–26:27"),))

=== 58.3 Discipline, environment and parental accountability
// NODE P13.C04.S03
#author("The Muslim Lantern")
// U ML022:482-540 | The Muslim Lantern | 5
#passage(("Then you have the idea of teaching them, having hobbies for them, having beneficial things for them. Try to make sure as much as you can that these hobbies are also social. Not to have everything they do is just live on a phone. Even if they go do some sort of a beneficial sport where they learn how to protect themselves or defend themselves. Beneficial hobbies, things which can benefit the kid, which you can also enjoy, but at the same time something that can bring them benefit. Physically, it can have them interact with other people. It can have them build character. So what you need to teach your kid: look for these types of hobbies that builds their character, rather than living and dying on their phone.", "And make sure they have the right environment, the right circle of friends. How do you know your kid is going to have the right circle of friends? By finding families which have the similar values as the ones you have. If another family has a similar value as the ones that you hold to and the ones that you have yourself, then it's okay for your kids to be around, because if you believe the right things and they have the same values, then their kids will be [upon] the right values and they can be around them. So you make sure that your kids are around the kids of other families that have similar values to the ones you have. And you make sure that they are not around the wrong friends. You tell them not to be with these friends, if they are wrong friends, if they're going to take them to the wrong path. You need to put effort as a parent to make sure what kind of friends your kids have.", "And, like I said, you cannot raise them in a non-Muslim environment. I was even telling to my friends: he takes his kids to a kindergarten, and at kindergarten they're already teaching them about rainbow things and issues from this age. Even kindergarten, let alone talking about elementary school or secondary school. Very important where you put your kids. So take your kids to the mosque with you. As a father, you go to the mosque and take your kid to the mosque. Take them to Islamic lectures, so they can listen and they learn from a young age, so they can grow up with this in their mind, rather than grow up in their mind watching Hollywood, watching this YouTuber, watching that streamer. If they grow up watching that streamer, they would want to be a streamer. If they grow up in the mosque, they grow up watching Islamic lecture, they grow up with their role models as the Sahaba, they would want to be like the Sahabah. So, pay attention to this. It's very important.", "And when you deal with your kids, this harshness of everything usually pushes your kid away. This is the fault that many parents do. You need to understand how to teach your kids the right way. You need to use motivation with them. Usually when kids are young, use motivational methods with them. Buy things that they love and desire, if you want them to do something, you want them to learn a story, you want them to come and sit and listen. When they do something good, reward them. Buy gifts. For example, if they're going to watch something beneficial and they like watching (maybe there is these cartoons that teaches you some morals or something like that; they like watching them), so, make it as a gift. Make it as a motivational thing. If they do the right thing, they watch. You bring them gifts, you bring them sweets, you bring them stuff like that. Take them on trips as a motivational thing when they do something right. You have to put an effort as a parent to motivate your kids in the right way.", "The firmness can be applied, and it has its own places, but firmness should only be applied when the matter is really serious. Talking about really serious issues: you do something really wrong and it needs to be corrected, then firmness can be applied. Like you change your tone of voice, you change your look when you look at them, you make sure that this is something serious. But if you're doing this all the time, then they wouldn't care. This should only be done when something is really serious, so they can understand, \"Okay, I've done something really serious.\" Because I'm always good, kind, merciful to my kids, but all of a sudden now I'm being very firm, they understand this is something very serious: \"I need to make sure not to do this again.\"",), "The Muslim Lantern, “If You're Doing This, There's A Chance Your Kid Leaves Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r632sKczYqY", ((1589, "26:29–27:09"), (1631, "27:11–27:52"), (1672, "27:52–28:49"), (1730, "28:50–29:41"), (1782, "29:42–30:21"),))
// U ML233:2216-2314 | The Muslim Lantern | 3
#passage(("Are you allowed to hit your children? That's a very excellent question. It's a very important question, because there's a lot of people who would exaggerate and do things that are not correct with their children. And I'll give an advice to all the parents: if you don't want your children to dislike Islam, don't be a means of pushing them away from it by making everything by force.", "Look at the prophet sallallahu [alaihi] wasallam, how he used to teach. When the prophet was on the camel, he said to him (look at this [ways] of teaching and explaining): \"Oh young boy, let me teach you a few words, let me teach you few beneficial words.\" He told: \"Ask Allah; if you seek help, ask from Allah alone. And if everyone gathers to harm you, they only going to harm you if something Allah has written upon you. And if they gather all to benefit you, they cannot benefit you except if Allah Wills to benefit you.\" These are deep Concepts. He very young boy, 10 years old or very young, but this was how the prophet dealt with the youngsters. The prophet was like this: joking around with the young children, being nice to them, teaching them, being open to them. That is a lot more effective.", "So discipline is allowed, level of discipline is allowed. There has to be sometimes a level of discipline as a punishment. But how do you discipline? There ways to discipline in which you deprive them from something they love, and that is far more painful than actual using a physical force. For example, the child loves his phone (which we shouldn't be doing anyways, but assuming he's doing it), and you take the phone away from him, or Internet away: that is far more effective of a form of deterrent than actually physical Force, I would say. So in Islam you're not allowed with anyone to severely hit, leave marks, break bones, [hit] the face, any of that. You shouldn't be doing that. Not allowed to harm anyone else or yourself. So this is not allowed with anyone in general, children otherwise, your own children, it doesn't matter. So there is a very small level of physical discipline that can be allowed. And, as I said, I wouldn't advise using that. It is not the most effective one, in our personal experience and in most people's personal experience.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Eddie @TheDeenShowTV Interviews The Muslim Lantern | Masjid Dar Al-Qur'an | Chicago - USA”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sLfJyJGj_eg", ((4887, "1:21:27–1:21:57"), (4919, "1:21:59–1:23:19"), (5031, "1:23:51–1:25:10"),))
// U ML022:541-579 | The Muslim Lantern | 3
#passage(("I'm telling you these things to do before it is too late. You will be responsible on the day of judgment for what your kids do if you don't teach them. If you don't take your responsibility and put the effort in and do everything in your power, Allah will hold you accountable for how they ended up. So, I'm telling you this before it's too late, before you become that person: \"Oh, let me book a call. Let me get a private session, because my son left Islam, or my daughter become like this, or my son has become like that.\" Well, they will become like this if you don't teach them the foundations. So, before it is too late, you got to wake up. You got to understand it is time now for us to put some effort as parents, to sit down seriously. Session I was having not too long ago: the parents were crying. They were sitting down like, \"We're realizing.\" But, realizing when? When it's too late? \"Oh, we should have been doing this. We realized that we were wrong.\" You cannot go back in time. So, do not be the parent who leaves his kids without guidance, without role models, without teachers, and not instilling the right values in them from a young age. Teach your kid the right beliefs, the right creed that we have as Muslims, the right behaviors, and have role models for them.", "And make sure these phones, these devices are not given. These devices should only be given for call. Give them a Nokia phone or something like that. When they're elder, once they have the correct foundations, the correct beliefs, you're sure that they're understanding, they're good, then maybe you can introduce these devices into the life. Never ever introduce these social media devices and phones and tablets for your kid when he's 7, 8, 9, 10, or 11 years old. Never. Wait until your kid has already been established in the right beliefs, in the right foundation, in the right understanding. He's smarter. He understands the right from wrong. Then perhaps you can gradually start to introduce these devices.", "And if you've already done this, then you have to slowly apply what we said you need to do, and slowly restrict that usage in their life. It'll be very difficult, because now you've made their life on their phone, because you were doing bad parenting. But if you want your kids to be good in the future, do not go in the complete path of destruction; start applying the right teachings. It will make an influence, even if your kids grew up with these things, slowly by slowly. Make a few restrictions on the usages, introduce the beliefs, them understanding what they believe. Even if they go astray a little bit, there's always a foundation they can come back to. May Allah Azza Wajjal guide us all and guide our kids, and guide our families to the best of mannerisms and best of deeds and that which pleases him.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “If You're Doing This, There's A Chance Your Kid Leaves Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r632sKczYqY", ((1822, "30:22–31:26"), (1886, "31:26–32:11"), (1933, "32:13–33:01"),))

=== 58.4 When a child leaves Islam: disowning and family conflict
// NODE P13.C04.S04
#author("The Muslim Lantern")
// U ML094:321-405 | The Muslim Lantern | 4
#passage(("He's a well-known YouTuber, I think, here in the UK. His name is Chunkz, and he's a Muslim YouTuber. There was a recent clip circulating around, where someone asked him about if his children leave Islam, what would he do, or something along those lines. There was a lot of controversy about this, I've seen. You have two sides: some people are completely criticising him for this, some others are completely in his favour and agree with what he said. What's the right position to take in this case? Is what he said right, or what he said is wrong?", "But before I say this, I want to say something very important. Just because someone is a celebrity or a popular figure, and he's a Muslim, it doesn't make him an authority over Islam. If he says something, he can say something which is wrong, which is his position. That doesn't represent the faith. It doesn't necessarily mean this is what Muslims believe. And what I respect in this video, that he didn't claim that. He didn't say Islam teaches me this, or teaches me that. All he said is \"for me\". So he was speaking from a very subjective point of view, about his personal position in decision making in case this were to happen.", "Now you had some disbelievers, not even Muslims, commenting on this and criticising him for doing it. Because he's a celebrity, he has a huge non Muslim followers, so all of those people are criticising him. But the funny thing is this: this is something that people who have no true purpose in life will never understand. If you are someone who just lives in this life for your own materialistic desires, for eating and drinking and making money and having intercourse, and all of that, you can never understand something like this. It's beyond your understanding. If you're not a religious person, you cannot comprehend a concept like this, or why would someone say something like this.", "But the double standard and hypocrisy, I would say, is that those people who are criticising him are non Muslims. Most of them are either liberals or conservatives in the West, non Muslims! So those same people will also disown their children for other reasons. For instance, if you have a conservative, they'll probably disown their child if their daughter, for instance, starts some adult content or whatever. They will disown her, because she's starting to do this adult content, because that's a red line for them as a conservative. Or if their son becomes LGBTQ or something like that, they'll disown that kid. So for them, many of these conservatives (I'm not saying all of them), this is where they will say: \"Oh, this is my red line. This is where I would disown my child.\" Let's say the liberals will say: \"I disown my child if he becomes an N-A-Z-I, for example, or supporter of those people, or he becomes an open racist; I'm going to disown my child!\" You'll have those two people having their own red lines, which is based on their own either liberal or conservative standpoints. And for them, that's OK. If someone were to say this, a lot of people would be OK: we don't mind! But if it comes to religion: oh, religion is just... you can do whatever you want! You can pick whatever you want and follow whatever you want! So that's something they're not going to understand. So this is my comment to those people who are not Muslims: it's something you cannot understand, because you're not even a religious person. You have your own red lines. You're in no position to come and criticise a Muslim for saying something like this.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Clip-React| Asmongold Wants To Read The Quran?! Chunkz Will Disown His Kids If They Leave Islam?!”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Y2zJLeRXXXk", ((949, "15:49–16:59"), (1022, "17:02–17:31"), (1056, "17:36–18:08"), (1091, "18:11–19:41"),))
// U ML094:436-545 | The Muslim Lantern | 4
#passage(("So what should we do as Muslims, if, let's say, I have a child, daughter or son, that decide to leave Islam? The initial first step is to understand: where does this come from? What's the cause? If you did your job as a parent, and taught your children the religion from the beginning, there MUST be something going on that you don't see. Now, most cases, actually, it's the fault of the PARENTS, because they didn't do enough effort teaching their children, don't see who the children are friends with, they take them to different places, they focus on other things rather than teaching their children their faith! But even if it is, or it isn't the fault of the parents, there's ALWAYS a cause. So you need to sit down in a merciful way with your child, and try to understand why they left the faith. And then you'll try to speak to them, answer their questions. If you're not qualified, you'd bring someone to answer the questions. You'll try with them in every possible way, and try to fix the problem with a loving attitude towards your child.", "Now, in the case that your child continues in this behaviour (now that's a very important thing to understand): because for Muslims, the connection of religion is stronger than the connection of blood. Allah says in the Quran (alHujurat49:10) (Verily, the believers are brothers). Every Muslim, 2 billion people, EVERY Muslim in the world is my brother or sister, stronger than the connection of blood that I would have with my own brother or my own sister. This is our tradition. This is what we believe as Muslims. So if that child, let's say, persists: you tried everything, they just don't want anything that has to do with the religion, they want to stay on disbelief, they are arrogantly remaining in that position, they don't care what you've done for them. Now, can you Islamically disown that child? Yes, you can! That is our red line as a Muslim. We can do this. And it's not only for leaving Islam. It could be other Major Sins, it could be for other major issues. And if they do that, you're allowed to do that. And when we say disown, it just depends on what the person means by disowning. We're saying removing communication with them, cutting communication with that person. Yes, that is your right! You are allowed to do that in Islam. Why? What's the point? Because you've tried everything with them. They're not listening, they're remaining in a path of evil. So you hope that this disconnection and disowning gives them a wake-up call. Let them reconsider their position. It's one of the methods of advising someone, and we do believe as Muslims that you're allowed to do that.", "And in the Quran (for instance, when I say this connection of blood versus the connection of religion), Allah gives us an example of Noah with his son. Allah told Noah that he's going to save his family (Hud11:40). And then when the flood happened, Noah called upon God (Hud11:45-46). And then Noah speaking to Allahﷻ and he said: (Oh Allah, my son is from my family) وَإِنَّ وَعۡدَكَ ٱلۡحَقُّ (And your promise is the Truth) وَأَنتَ أَحۡكَمُ ٱلۡحَـٰكِمِینَ (And you're the Wisest of Judges). So Noah 'alayhi salam (peace be upon him) called upon God, and he said: Oh Allah, that my son is from my family, and now he's going to drown, and you promised me that you're going to save my family. Then Allah responded to him: إِنَّهُۥ لَیۡسَ مِنۡ أَهۡلِكَۖ إِنَّهُۥ عَمَلٌ غَیۡرُ صَـٰلِحࣲۖ Allah said: (He is not from your family. He is unrighteous deed). That's how Allahﷻ described his blood son, if you want to use that blood relationship. So when Allah says he's not from your family, meaning he's not from your religion, from your faith. He is unrighteous deed, meaning he committed disbelief: he associated with God, he worshipped other than God, he disbelieved in the Creator. He's not from your family anymore. He's not from this connection that you have, that we described, that is thicker than blood. So to us as Muslims, yes, the religion is thicker than blood.", "So what Chunkz was saying, like I said, it needed a little bit of refinement. Explaining at first: no, you will take a merciful stance with your child. First, try to recognise the reason, because there's a lot of miscommunication, there's a lot of ignorance, there's a lot of doubts that are spread around that can confuse someone. But once someone does everything possible and tries his best with his son or daughter, and they still remain on arrogance and disbelief, yes, that is something you should do as a Muslim, actually, as a form of admonishing and giving advice to that kid, hoping that they will wake up and they come back to the path of the Truth. Because to us as Muslims, if they die as disbelievers, then they've lost everything, and eternality of Hellfire. Me disowning him or not disowning him: if that helps my child, he's not going to spend an eternality in Hellfire, yes! I would do that. Absolutely. And this is our red line, like I said. So may Allahﷻ guide us all, and inshaAllah keep us all firm on the Truth. And if this were to happen with you or your family members or your friends, take the right approach. Don't take the wrong approach in the beginning, because I know many communities and societies, they will instantly go to this point, and that can have more of a negative effect on the child, because they're confused, they're doubtful, there's an issue. Rather than taking it in a merciful, lenient way, that actually will bring them closer, you actually push them further away from the Faith.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Clip-React| Asmongold Wants To Read The Quran?! Chunkz Will Disown His Kids If They Leave Islam?!”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Y2zJLeRXXXk", ((1269, "21:09–22:05"), (1330, "22:10–23:35"), (1418, "23:38–24:52"), (1496, "24:56–26:20"),))
