// U ML299:1-92 | The Muslim Lantern | 2
#passage(("I like your mother, honestly, from what you said about her. I think she's a very nice person, nice individual, and I'll explain why. So I think if I live under my parents' roof, they can make the rules. This is something I would say personally. I'll follow the rules, because I'm living under their rule. I'm living under their house. And I know that my mother and my father, they only want good for me, no matter what anyone says. Anyone who comes and claims a mother doesn't love her daughter or doesn't love her son is someone who knows nothing about motherhood and fatherhood. I know for a fact your mother wants good for you. Of course she's not happy with what you're doing, because she knows there's consequences. She knows Islam is the Truth, she knows there's punishment, whether you care or you don't care, with all due respect to you.", "You are using feelings, you're not using facts. When you say, for example, \"I don't feel like it is the thing for me\", because you want to dance and you want to... what you call \"express yourself\", you want to do these type of things: it does not feel it's for you, but the Truth is not based on feelings. Truth is Truth. So if she's more wise than you (I'm not saying she is or she isn't, I'm saying but if she is more wise than you), and she knows this is a Truth, and based on her knowing this is a Truth, she wants good for you, she doesn't want her daughter to be punished, she doesn't want her daughter to be led astray. What might appear to you as harshness is more of care, is more of wanting good for you, is more of looking after her own daughter. If she doesn't tell you anything, that is a sign that she doesn't care. Because if she doesn't care about you, then: yeah, do whatever you want to do, just go do this or do that; she's not going to care what you're doing. But this is a sign that she cares about you. The amount of effort that she's putting in to help you is just a sign of how much she actually cares about you as her daughter, how much she wants good for you.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Ex-Muslim Daughter Questioning Her Mom's Decisions! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2QWT9LbSQy4", ((226, "3:46–4:36"), (276, "4:36–5:29"),))
// U ML299:128-157 | The Muslim Lantern | 1
#passage(("Say hypothetically you had a child, and that child is running towards the fire: what would you do? What if they're fighting you? Would you use a level of force to stop them? Absolutely. So the level of restriction is based on the level of resistance. That's essentially what your mother's doing. She sees you are butting heads with her, as we say, and she's increasing her (whatever word you want to use) restraint, because she sees your level of rebellions, if you want to use that term. So she knows what is good for you. The child might think: you know what? This is light and it looks very beautiful and orange and all of these different colors. And he wants to touch it, and he thinks this is a good thing, he wants to do it, and then he thinks the parent is evil because the parent is pushing him away from the fire. Or that child wants to eat so many sweets and the parent is stopping them from doing it, because it's not good for their teeth, it's not good for their blood level or any of that. So you have a perspective of your mother. I think that you should love your mother for what she's doing; that's my honest opinion. Because it shows she cares.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Ex-Muslim Daughter Questioning Her Mom's Decisions! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2QWT9LbSQy4", ((503, "8:23–9:33"),))
// U ML299:211-251 | The Muslim Lantern | 1
#passage(("I do hope you also don't develop a resistance for Islam because of what your mother is doing, because that subconsciously can happen. What could be a reason for you to push yourself away from Islam is the fact that your mother is pushing you towards Islam. So I think it's very important for you to keep it in mind: that could be an effect that is taking place and you're not noticing it. And trust me, the evidences for why Islam is the Truth: when you talk about rationality, I think anyone who's sincere with himself, you cannot argue against the evidences that Islam is The Truth; it's impossible. Now feelings is a different issue. No one can control your feelings or your heart. It's something that is not in my hands. But to show rationally why Islam is a truth, that is the easiest thing to do.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Ex-Muslim Daughter Questioning Her Mom's Decisions! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2QWT9LbSQy4", ((859, "14:19–14:59"),))
// U ML346:132-160 | The Muslim Lantern | 1
#passage(("So there are boundaries in honouring your parents. And I would say, the boundary is when things are against God, or when things are in line with what God taught. How much should you honour your parents? You honour your parents as long as it's in line with the teachings of the Creator. And that's the teachings of Islam! If someone were to accept Islam and his parents are not Muslims, he still has to be their companion and be good to them in this life, even though they're not Muslims. But he doesn't obey them in anything that goes against God. So, that's the limit.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Christian Challenges Muslim On Biblical Authority! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_1T5OxDHRWA", ((440, "7:20–7:44"),))

=== 58.5 The marriage crisis: the role of parents
// NODE P13.C04.S05
#author("The Muslim Lantern")
// U ML009:1-198 | The Muslim Lantern | 14
#passage(("We have a serious crisis in the Muslim community today, and this crisis is not being talked about enough, and that is the marriage crisis. There's general overall marriage crisis in the world today, but especially within the Muslim community. And I get a lot of messages from brothers and sisters alike that they're doing many things wrong, or they have done many things wrong, and they're struggling to get married. Now some of them haven't done anything wrong, but they're also struggling to get married. So everyone is struggling to get married. It's very important that we highlight these issues that we have as a community, so we can attempt to fix it, so we can attempt to stop contributing towards this crisis that is causing a lot of damage and harm in our community. And the people who are causing this crisis are the parents, the women that are trying to get married and the men that are trying to get married.", "The prophet, there's a hadith where he says: if someone comes to you and you accept his religion, he has a good religion, he has good manners, then get him married. If you do not do this, then there will be great corruption in the land. Now there are some scholars that weaken this narration, and there is a debate over the authenticity of it. However, its meaning is for sure correct. If marriage stops becoming about someone has good religion and good characteristics and worthy of marriage, and starts becoming about other worldly matters and worldly things, then great corruption will spread, because if people do not get married then they are going to resort to haram.", "So what are the issues? What are we doing to contribute towards this crisis that we have? I'm going to start talking about the parents. Why am I going to start talking about the parents? Because the parents are the core. If the parents are doing things correctly, then that will influence their children, and by extension that will solve majority of the issues that we're having today. However, a lot of these issues are related to the parents.", "The first thing I'm going to talk about is the idea of raising your kids. The parents are not raising their kids, teaching them the importance of marriage and family. They're not teaching them the necessary skills and the necessary things that they would need so they can be a desirable spouse, man or woman. The parents are not teaching the men to be responsible, to be strong, to be intelligent, to be able to protect and provide, to have the correct attributes of men, which is to keep your word, not to be a liar, to be courageous. We're not teaching our kids that. And we're not teaching them the importance of marriage as an idea: how important is it in Islam, or in religion, or in your life, to have one of your priorities to get married as soon as you can, so you can protect yourself from the [fitnah]. The parents are not doing this job of teaching the importance and the necessary skills. A lot of the sisters today and a lot of the brothers today do not have the skills. This is a massive problem that we have.", "And what are the parents focusing on? They're focusing on what the west have told them. This piece of paper: everything is about education. Why? Because [Allah] says on the Quran this life, this is about mutual [boasting]. My son is an engineer. My daughter is a doctor. My son is this. My daughter is that. Parents care about [boasting] themselves to other parents rather than caring about what is the benefit and what is important for the future of the children. And this applies both to men and women. However, it's more important in the case of the girls.", "Now another very important thing that the parents are doing, which is horrendous, which is the idea of stopping their kids from getting married when they're young. I get many messages from sisters and brothers alike. They want to get married. They're young. They can be in their mid to late teens or they can be in early 20s. Whatever their age is, they want to get married. They have a desire. And the parents are just like, \"You're too young.\" And it's good sisters and good brothers, and they seem to be willing to get married. They're mature enough to get married from how they speak. However, the parents are like, \"Oh, no, no, you're young.\" Some parents, they just want to keep their kids with them for longer. Some of the parents are just careless: I don't want to deal with all of these issues. I'm just going to say you're young. Come back later.", "And the [prophet] he said, \"Oh young people, whoever of you is able to get married, let him get married.\" Why did they say, \"Oh young people\" specifically? And then the [prophet] he said: who cannot, let him fast, because it's going to be protecting him from what? From the desire. The prophet is giving this advice to the young men and women because the prophet knows that the desire is there in this specific age; the desire is more amplified than anything else. So when you go tell your kid, \"Oh, you're too young. Just go,\" later you come and say, \"Why is my daughter with this random boyfriend, or my son is dating this girl?\" Why? Because the kid wants to get married. You have not talked seriously with your kid. You have not put the effort to get them married when they're young. All what you're thinking about is like, \"I don't want to deal with this right now. Couldn't care less.\" Focus on your own self, as if your children are not important. So you are contributing towards this crisis that we have, because you are preventing a lot of these men and women from getting married in a age when they are desiring the marriage, and then they end up getting attached in haram relationships, and years and years go by, and then they don't get married, and they start having trauma, and we have a million issue in the community just because you are preventing your own kids from getting married.", "Then we have the idea of asking ridiculous amounts of money when the man is coming for your daughter: okay, go bring me 100, 200k, bring me a car; complicating everything in the marriage: I want this wedding to be like this and that. And all of it is coming from what we said about [boasting], this mutual [boasting]: oh, your cousin, she had the biggest wedding in the entire city, so you need to have a similar wedding, or you shouldn't get married. For them, they don't care: the husband could later on do all types of things that are wrong to the girl. But as long as he does the stupid wedding and they can [boast] to their other stupid people in the community, then they're happy. This is one of the most serious issues which prevents people from getting married.", "I'm talking about my experience with a lot of men and women that are struggling to get married, and what they say is the issues that they're facing. A man was trying to get married. He's not poor and he's not rich, he's okay. However, in some communities, with gold and silver, they're asking him for this, asking, and the brother is struggling to get married. He can't get married. He can take care of the family, he can look after his kids; however, he cannot fulfill these ridiculous requirements to do with weddings and to do with money and to do with all of these things that this person did and you're not doing. So these parents need to wake up. You want to sell your daughter as a piece of clothing. You want someone that destroys the life of your daughter later on, just because you are able to [boast] for another parent. So you got to wake up as parents. You got to put the priority, the mannerisms, the [deen] of the individual that is coming to marry your daughter before everything else. That is the first thing that you need to put.", "Now parents as well, both father and the mother, are not putting effort as well to find spouses for their kids. They're not putting any effort trying to see in the community if there is people that they know in the similar age, and they probably know many families that have people in similar ages. However, they're not putting effort. This is general, but it's specifically about the fathers as well, because it's the father's job to put effort to find someone for his daughter. And then the sisters, they come later and they speak to me, and they're like in their 30s or whatever, going to get married, and say, \"Does your father put any effort?\" \"No, he didn't really. He wasn't concerned.\" So, the fathers are not even putting any effort. They're expecting their daughter to go and find some random man in the street and come like, \"Okay, here, I want to marry this person.\" What, are we going to pretend that we're [kuffar]? We're going to deal like them, or what? It is your responsibility as a father to put effort.", "Of course, it is permissible for a Muslim woman, if she finds the right person, that somehow she reaches out in an indirect way and tries to get him to speak to her father. I'm not saying this is haram, so long as it's within Islamic bounds and is done correctly. However, that is not what majority of sisters are going to do, number one; and number two, that is not their responsibility in the first place, but it is mainly the responsibility of the father to put that effort and try and find for his daughters. We know in the Quran [Musa alaihi salam], when he was presented, he told him, \"I will give you one of my two daughters in marriage.\" Why is he presenting his daughters? He sees a man who's worthy. [Musa alaihi salam is] a prophet of god; he's worthy, and he has right character, right religion. So he is presenting his daughters, because that is his responsibility as a father. The daughters didn't come and say, \"Oh, we want to marry you.\" They came, they said, \"Our father, he wants something from you. He wants to have a word with you.\" That's it. They connected him, and then he spoke to them. So that is your responsibility as a father. Don't just sit down and wait like this, and then tomorrow you're going to come and cry when your daughter is going with this guy, with Max, with Jonathan, with whatever, behind the bush. It's your fault. You're not putting any effort. The desire is there at the kids, and you're not doing any work as parents to get your children married in the right age, when they have that desire, when they are looking for marriage.", "Now also you have the idea of the parents and many communities putting the culture over the religion: \"Oh, we're only going to marry from our cousin's family or this family or this ethnicity.\" They would reject people: \"Oh, this person is a revert. I'm going to reject him. Oh, this man is from Somalia or [Ethiopia] or whatever. I'm going to reject him.\" Doesn't matter if he has the right religion, the right character, whatever. No, it's about a lot of racism and a lot of cultural ignorance that are being applied, and it is preventing a lot of good marriages, even though the sisters agree and the brothers agreeing, but the parents are the ones who are coming and preventing that marriage for all of these cultural reason. Many of the scholars have advised against very close marriage, which you have in many communities, rather than branching out and looking for who is the most suitable. They just want to own: \"It has to be from our tribe.\" No. Allah says he made us into different nations and tribes so we can get to know each other. [The Prophet] he said there's no superiority of a white over a black or a black over white. It's about your piety. This is what you need to look for.", "Also that parents putting their ego over the benefits of the kids. Sometimes there's a good man and a good woman. They want to get married and they're both agreeing, and they're both okay, everything is good, but this parent has an issue with the parent of the boy, or this mother has an issue with the other mother. The marriage is not about you, it's about them. If they're agreeing with each other, they both have good character and religion, fix your issues. Put your step on your ego and put the benefit of your children first.", "So these are, I would say, the main issues that I'm seeing. There's of course other issues, but these are some of the main issues that are to do with the parents specifically: what we are doing as parents to contribute more and more towards this crisis. I want you to understand this crisis, it destroys communities. Because when we don't have families, which is the foundation of the community, the foundation of everything, then you don't have the kids being raised correctly. You have everybody doing haram. You have children born out of wedlock. You have people caring about the [dunya]. We have nothing. We have no community. If we don't have a family, we don't have a community. So, we're talking about something serious that we are contributing, and every day we're leading into more and more problems in our community. We need to fix this. We need to work on this.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “The Muslim Marriage Crisis: Causes, Effects & Solutions | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=03ZEqe5zZFo", ((0, "0:00–0:43"), (46, "0:46–1:30"), (90, "1:30–1:54"), (118, "1:58–2:55"), (175, "2:55–3:27"), (236, "3:56–4:38"), (278, "4:38–5:56"), (356, "5:56–6:44"), (406, "6:46–7:43"), (465, "7:45–8:36"), (516, "8:36–10:03"), (603, "10:03–11:03"), (663, "11:03–11:26"), (686, "11:26–12:09"),))

=== 58.6 The marriage crisis: delayed marriage and expectations
// NODE P13.C04.S06
#author("The Muslim Lantern")
// U ML009:199-253 | The Muslim Lantern | 5
#passage(("What are the main issues that we see with the sisters in the community that are also contributing to this marriage crisis that we're dealing with? The first thing I'm going to highlight is the idea of unrealistic expectations. And a lot of it is influenced by social media. A lot of sisters are on social media. They watch a lot of nonsense. They watch a lot of [series], dramas, follow this actress, this person, that person. And their expectation is, \"Oh, I want to go to the Hawaii. I want a guy that looks like Brad [Pitt]. I want him to be like this. I want him to be like that.\" All of these nonsensical, unrealistic expectations.", "They don't look at someone who's in the same level as themselves. They think they can get something that doesn't exist, only exists in movies. A lot of sisters have this issue. But not only that, it's also because we get a lot of women on social media and you get a lot of stupid men messaging them and showing desire in them. So they think that there's millions of options out there. And majority of these are a bunch of losers, of course. They wouldn't be doing this unless they're a bunch of losers. And also, they're not interested in marrying you and taking care of you. None of that. They just want to do a specific thing. But it gives this wrong perception in the mind of women: oh, there's a million men I can choose from. And these are all men they wouldn't want to marry anyways. But it gives them this issue which [feeds] their ego and gives them this unrealistic expectations where they think they can marry the president of the country.", "No, we got to look for what is suitable for us, and our priority should be the [deen], should be the religion, should be the mannerisms, should be the person that fears Allah, because if he fears Allah, he will fear Allah in dealing with you. He will look after you, he'll take care of you. He wouldn't do something bad to you because he fears Allah and he cares about his religion and he has morals. That should be your priority, not the other unrealistic expectations that you are influenced by watching social media.", "Now also the idea which upsets many people: the idea of the women putting their career or education as a priority over marriage. Nobody's telling you don't get educated ever. This piece of paper is not the foundation of your life. I get many messages from sisters that are 34, 35 and they all talk about how much they regret that they haven't started seeking marriage earlier, and they left everything just focusing on career, focusing on this, focusing on that, and now they cannot find a spouse, now they cannot get married because they didn't put it as a priority.", "If all your energy is being put towards career and education and you don't have marriage at all in your mind, you will end up wasting years of your life, and in the end it is going to be very difficult for you to get married. So this idea of putting the career first: we're not telling you do not get educated, do not work in a halal way with the Islamic requirement. Nobody's saying this. However, we're talking about priorities. They don't have to be against each other. But a priority should be marriage over education. A priority should be marriage over a career, because you will want it sooner or later. However, it will not be easier for you to do later on.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “The Muslim Marriage Crisis: Causes, Effects & Solutions | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=03ZEqe5zZFo", ((734, "12:14–12:50"), (770, "12:50–13:50"), (830, "13:50–14:15"), (855, "14:15–14:55"), (898, "14:58–15:37"),))
// U ML196:149-215 | The Muslim Lantern | 5
#passage(("Her priority shouldn't be the priority of a lot of women in the West: the feministic priority of my career. And then they say, for instance, \"I'm going to start thinking about marriage when I'm 35.\" And in reality, you're going to start thinking about marriage when you're 35, but you're not going to be desired by the opposite gender when you're 35. And they don't realise that, because they think men and women are the same thing in their mind: \"Oh, yeah, I like men that are older than me\", therefore, men will like women that are older than them! No, it doesn't work like that. Because we're opposite genders, we have different attractions. The men would always be attracted to girls that are younger (we're speaking in general, of course) and women will be attracted to older. And that is where the harmony comes in. If both of them are attracted to older, the society will not even function!", "When it comes to fertility, these are biological things. A woman hits the age of 30, 80% of her eggs are gone; when she's 35, 90%. And then you start going into high-risk pregnancy, in which there is damage; it can be on the child or the mother's health. These are biological realities. Someone might be, \"Oh, I don't like this\", but I'm sorry, this is the truth. This is the reality. I cannot change human biology reality for you.", "So they put their career first. And they spend their life doing that thing, working, stressing, completely miserable! And they reach an age, sadly, in which they realise that's too late, maybe in the end of the 20s, beginning of 30s. And I've seen a lot of applications for people who try to get married. And I've seen always a common theme. The overwhelming majority of the women that are not married, are trying to get married, are at the end of their 20s, beginning of their 30s plus. There are websites, there are apps, there are mosques, where people sometimes put resumes; they're trying to get married. So they try to connect people with each other. So I'm talking about looking at this data, looking at this information, the people that I interact with generally; this has always been the case.", "And these women will have degrees actually, a lot of them will have degrees, but sometimes the degree can actually be an obstacle. Because when a woman reaches a certain level of education and intelligence, also the type of partner that she looks for changes. She wants someone who's more intelligent than herself, someone who can actually engage in a conversation, tell her information she doesn't know, has more experience than herself. And if this is the case, then doors start closing for her for the other types of people that have not had this level of education, do not have a master's degree, do not have a PhD, whatever she did in her higher education.", "So all of these things are just facts and realities. We're not telling anyone what to do. People will make their own decisions in life. We're telling them what we see, how the reality actually works. Someone can say, \"Oh, no, I don't care.\" Go ahead, seek all the higher education you want to seek. Become the most knowledgeable, intelligent person and put your career as a priority. That's up to you. In the end, we're giving advice for people who are looking for advice.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Agnostic Questions Muslim On The Education Of Women In Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K1ADYkUV9-Q", ((434, "7:14–8:00"), (486, "8:06–8:30"), (516, "8:36–9:47"), (592, "9:52–10:29"), (636, "10:36–11:06"),))
// U ML009:254-385 | The Muslim Lantern | 8
#passage(("Now, a lot of what sisters are being told is, \"Oh, I'm just going to care about marriage and I'm not going to work and I'm not going to get money. What if I get divorced? What's going to happen to me?\" This is the most popular idea that is being pushed in the minds of our sisters, that is preventing a lot of them from focusing on marriage. Now nobody enters something with the intention of failing in it. We don't say, \"What if I get in an accident? So I'm not going to get in a car because I could get in an accident. I'm not going to get in a plane because I could get in an accident. I'm not going to study and go into the exam because I could fail.\" There is risk in everything in life. Take the right precautions and find the right spouse and do whatever is in your capacity and be a righteous wife and rely on Allah. Where is Allah in the picture? Where is the reliance on Allah? Isn't [Allah] there to protect you?", "Also, what is this idea as if we don't have families? As if you don't have a brother, as if you don't have a father who can look after you as well. As if, if you got divorced, everyone else disappears. No, people don't disappear. You have a family that can look after you. But most important thing, you have [Allah]. You can get educated then get married. You can be married and work in halal and save money. You could work with your husband and make money, and if something happens, you have your money. It's not for him to take. However, that shouldn't be the reason we say, \"I'm not going to get married because what if I get divorced?\" This \"what if\" opens the door of the shaitan, as [the Prophet] said. It's just a trick of the shaitan that is being played now. So we need to start waking up and understanding that marriage should be put as a priority. It is something that [Allah] says in the Quran: he's the one who created for you from yourself spouses so you can have this peace and tranquility, and he made between you mercy and love. This is our nature, both as men and women. We can all pretend, \"Oh, I don't want to get married.\" We're just tricking yourself. You will come and say, when it is too late, \"I want to get married, I feel alone.\" But you need to start doing this earlier.", "Now a lot of these sisters will have [bitter] sisters around them, wrong friend group. Why are they [bitter]? Because many of these women have done many haram things and had many bad experiences with men. They're unable to get married, and they say misery loves company. So they do two things. First, they want to keep you from getting married, because then they feel like, \"It's not only me. I'm not getting married, you're also not getting married.\" And a lot of women are envious like that. They don't want you to get married so they can feel better about themselves. This idea of trying to make more people like themselves. The second thing that they're doing is giving you all of these stories: \"Oh, look, I got married and this happened, and this woman got married and this happened to her. So don't get married because this may happen to you as well.\" So they start making them hate marriage. And a lot of sisters, what they do is they generalize the personal experiences: if this happened to someone, then it will happen to me as well. No, there is bad experiences in everything in life. If, just because a person had bad experience in something, they were not going to do it, nobody's going to do anything. So it's very important that you look at the friends in your group. Are they encouraging you to get married, to find happiness, or are they envious of you and they're trying to put you down and bring you to the same company that they have?", "And then you have this idea of \"I want to find myself\" phase. It's like I'm lost in the kitchen and I'm gonna go find myself. It is widespread. \"I have issues I need to work on. I'm not going to get married until I fix myself.\" Nobody is perfect. Nobody gets married and they have the perfect personality or everything goes well. Everyone, when they get married, you will have something that you don't like in your spouse. Every spouse will have issues. And that is the reality of life. You're not going to find yourself because you are yourself. So there is nothing to find. If by finding yourself you mean that you're going to fix every issue that you have, it will never happen. Everybody has issues. So, it's about being cautious of the issues that you have, working on yourself, which everybody does, and being honest with whoever the partner is that you're going to marry of the issues that you have, so they can be upfront as well with you and both of you can be on the same page, and then that doesn't lead to future issues.", "Now, a lot of women as well are being sold this idea, \"Oh, you're too young to get married.\" They're told by friends or whatever. [The Prophet] said, \"Oh young people, whoever of you is able to get married, let him get married.\" And a lot of sisters don't like hearing this, but we say to them that if you want the best chance to get the best spouse, to get the most amount of people that you can get married to, so you can get the best spouse, you can be happier in your life, then you need to look earlier, because it takes time to find people, especially in this day and age, and because when men look for women, they look for women that are in a childbearing age. When men come and I ask, \"What are you looking for?\" they say, \"Childbearing age.\" In other words, a sister that is younger in her age. A lot of sisters may be listening and they're older in age and they're not happy listening to this. It's the reality. Whether we like the reality or we don't like the reality, that is the reality. A man wants to get married. He wants to have kids, wants to have healthy kids. He wants his wife to have an easier pregnancy. It's natural. He want his wife to be younger so she can look after the kids and have more energy around the kids.", "Now if you as a sister did not get married and you're older, nobody is saying that you're not going to get married. You will find someone, inshallah. However, I'm talking about how likely you are and how easier it is and why you need to look when you're younger and leave this idea of, \"Oh, I'm too young to get married.\" No, you're not too young. As long as you are in an age where you have desires, there is a fitna. If you're not getting married, you will get all sorts of wolves around you trying to get to know you, and one day you will fall for the trick. You didn't get married, you'll end up doing something else, something haram, because everybody has desires, men and women.", "Then you have the opposite of that: you have some women telling girls, \"Oh, you got to start dating so you can find the husband. You're wearing a hijab, take it back a little bit. Show some of your hair so then you can find the husband. Take your hijab completely off so you can start attracting the opposite gender. Start going to the club and the bar and this person and this place and that place so you can find the husband. You're not going to find the husband by being a righteous sister.\" This is being widespread. That's the other extreme that we see in the community. And yeah, you will attract a lot more men like this, but you're going to attract the men that don't want to marry you. You're going to attract the men that you don't want to marry. You're going to attract the men that want to use you and move on to the next one. You're going to attract trauma. You're going to attract baggage. You're going to attract everything evil, because you use something that Allah is not pleased with to seek marriage, and you will end up regretting everything you do.", "There's so many experiences I can talk about, sisters coming. Remember the sister that came, she's a [revert], she brought a person with her. They were dating before marriage and she became pregnant, and that's why now they want to get married, and I said to her not to marry him. I told her you regret this. She came two years later and they divorced, and now she has a kid. She got married while she had a chance not to get married, but now she got married and wasted years from her life. Now she has the kid and she's divorced. Because you start with something haram, you build a relationship with someone in a haram way, they're not going to fear Allah with you because you started with them in the wrong way.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “The Muslim Marriage Crisis: Causes, Effects & Solutions | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=03ZEqe5zZFo", ((937, "15:37–16:32"), (992, "16:32–17:50"), (1070, "17:50–19:04"), (1144, "19:04–20:11"), (1214, "20:14–21:29"), (1291, "21:31–22:07"), (1328, "22:08–23:04"), (1384, "23:04–23:43"),))

=== 58.7 The marriage crisis: men's responsibilities and conduct
// NODE P13.C04.S07
#author("The Muslim Lantern")
// U ML009:420-571 | The Muslim Lantern | 9
#passage(("Now coming to the men, because of course it's not only the parents, it's not only the women, but a lot of the men, a lot of what they're doing is also contributing and being one of the main reasons why we have issues in the community. And we have a crisis. Now I'm going to say the same thing that I said about the women first for the men, which is the idea of high expectations. You have men that have no intelligence, no money, no ability, protection or provision, absolute fat, everything, and they want to marry Angelina [Jolie]. They say, \"Oh, I can't find the wife.\" It's not like you can't find the wife. You are chasing something way out of your league or way out of what you can get. And it's a reality.", "I remember this story of one of our teachers, where there was a young person, and he was telling the [sheikh], \"I'm struggling to get married. I can't find any woman.\" [He] told him, \"Really? You can't find a woman?\" He said, \"Yes.\" Said, \"Okay, I have a sister that is a widow that is ready to get married.\" He's like, \"No, no, no, no.\" [He] told him, \"It's not you're struggling to get married, are you? If you want to protect yourself from the fitna, then it's not about the age of the woman. If you want to get married and you feel you have a desire and you fear haram for yourself, then get married.\" There's multitude of different sisters that want to get married. But you want something specific. And in most cases, what you want is outside of your capacity.", "Some men as well, they want the sister that is [chaste], and at the same time she puts all types of makeup on. They want this contradiction. They want the woman that looks like the woman they see in the movies, the western woman, at the same time she's the most obedient, mashallah, [practising] in religion. You're not going to find that. You're not going to find two opposites together. You need to rewire your brain. This issue of indecent [porn] materials that the men are watching are completely destroying the community, that is making your attraction to the wrong type of women, that is contributing to a lot of the women doing the wrong things, because they want to be that type which you're attracted to, and both of them are just doing haram. No marriage is in the picture. So the men need to rewire their brain. They need to start seeking religion and the character of a woman first, and they need to look for their expectations where they are.", "Now also the idea of immaturity. There is extreme immaturity within the men. Muslim men, they're not working on themselves. They're not working on their characters. They don't have the masculine attributes of honesty, of working hard, of being firm, of being courageous. They're sitting playing video games 24/7 like a bunch of goofs and wasting their time on games and jumping up and down, and that's it. And then, \"Oh, I'm struggling to get married.\" Yeah, because you're a little boy, you're sitting playing on video games. Of course, you're struggling to get married. The minute you man up and you start becoming mature and you start developing your character, your intelligence, and yourself as a person, then it would be easier for you to find a spouse. But that spouse is not going to look for an idiot who's sitting in his basement getting obese and becoming 400 kg and playing video games and has no clue about how the world functions. His mommy and daddies are still feeding him in the basement and he's immature and he's saying, \"I'm struggling to get married.\" You are struggling to get married because you're not working on yourself. You need to start working on yourself.", "And that is linked now to the idea of laziness. Men being lazy, not willing to make halal money. They're not willing to go and work and be a provider for their families. They make all sorts of excuses: \"In this economy, you need to have women and men both working.\" You are a liar. Wallah, you are a liar. That's all I'm going to say. You are a liar and you're a coward, not willing to go and work. I know a brother who, alhamdulillah, he's married. He has three kids. May Allah bless him and continue to provide for him. And he only works in his family. He's in the UK. Look at how garbage the economy in the UK is. And he's one person working for years, looking out for his family and three kids alone. Allah is providing for him because he's putting his effort in his working. He's not doing any crazy jobs. He's doing sometimes delivery. He's doing this and that. He's doing the most of the basic jobs out there, but he's being intelligent about his spending. He's being intelligent about his wealth, and he's doing what is in his power, and he's relying on Allah. You're not doing that. So this excuse of \"this economy\": no, you are not putting the effort. You're being lazy, sitting playing video games, sitting in your comfort zone and expecting everything to happen.", "You've not been taught to be a man. I started working when I was 14 years old. I started working because my father taught me that: go make a living. Go make an earning. Learn how to be a man. Look how you're going to be treated by other people in work. They will do all types of things. They will compete with you to get your money. They will try to trick you. They don't care. You will learn, you gain experience how to be a man, how cruel the world can be, from a young age. I left the house, my parents' house. I was like 19 or something when I left the house. You need to start working on yourself. You need to start manning up, and the parents need to push you towards that.", "This idea of being lazy and \"I don't know how to make money\": the [rizq] is out there, especially in this day and age. There's nothing easier. We have kids 19 years old millionaires, from playing video games, and you're making whatever excuse there is that you're not able to make money. You are able to make money. I've seen very intelligent kids. I've had a shorts edit