eing married and waiting, it is fine. I said to one person I was talking to before: I don't believe Lesbians are a real thing. This is just my personal opinion, but I don't think they are a real thing, and there are always reasons for why it happens. And there are always ways to talk about these things with the right people who are Muslims (I'm not talking about Non-Muslim people); I am talking about Muslims who understand these issues, who can work them through and lead an individual into a more Islamically natural way of being or existing, the way that Allah created us.", "Label who? Are you going to have an ID that says you are \"Muslim This\"? This is a problem, I think, with the Western society today: people talk about these things, which are supposed to be private things, openly, as if they're their normal day-to-day talk. A person's sexual desires: these are private matters. When you say \"label\", that's something which is public, that people come across. People are not going to say, \"What's your name? What's your sexual orientation?\" That's not how people do it. So, unless they ask for it, you're not going to say. That's the first part. The second part is you shouldn't be labelling yourself with something that's considered a Sin, like: \"OK, السلام عليكم, my name is Muhammad the murderer!\" I'm not going to be labelling myself with something that is considered Islamically a Sin. It's not the right thing to do. Anything which is considered a Sin, no one should use that as a title and label himself as that thing. This is why I said, in the beginning, submitting to the idea that this is something which is Immoral: if I submit to it, then I don't want anything to do with it. I'm not proud of it.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Ex-Muslim Girl Leaves Islam For LGBTQ!? Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ygry-0BaOFo", ((127, "2:07–3:04"), (189, "3:09–4:09"), (307, "5:07–5:47"), (356, "5:56–6:09"), (409, "6:49–7:53"), (507, "8:27–9:30"),))

