// U ML289:307-397 | The Muslim Lantern | 7
#passage(("If you were to think about it, just from a purely rational perspective: is it beneficial for the society, for the community? If we look at it from an Atheistic perspective, for example, from a scientific perspective, they claim they believe in Evolution. The predominant view in the scientific world is that they believe in the theory of Evolution, and they say evolution is about survival and reproduction. Is it for survival and reproduction to be men being with [men]? So from their worldview, from the scientific worldview, that's not a good thing.", "Second thing is: are there diseases that are specifically linked to homosexual intercourse? Not Muslims are warning, but the same countries, the governments that allow these things, are the ones warning from it. For example, in the UK, you have the NHS, National Health Service. I believe they had put around 13 diseases that spread from a male to male intercourse, homosexual intercourse, and they put five to six, if I remember, from lesbian intercourse, female to female. And why do they say these diseases come from that specific type of intercourse? I don't want to be graphic here. Someone is having intercourse with another individual where that individual literally excretes. So that place where you are having intercourse with that individual in, it's a place for out, not for in; that's number one. And number two: it's a dirty place, for the fact that it is a place where you excrete from. Thirdly, because it's not prepared for that, there's a lot of ruptures that happen when that type of intercourse takes place, and because rupturing happens and then bleeding takes place, then all of these disgusting diseases come about. Also there's a lot of oral things that these people do. That's why you find all of these diseases. Islam does not allow these types of things, these type of behaviours that they do.", "So what we say as Muslims is: it's basic common sense, these things are causing damage. And then when someone 