#passage(("Let's talk today. You're talking about back in history; that's neither here or there, because we cannot prove it. If I can say, for example, there was tons of books about X and they were burned, I cannot essentially prove that claim, because it's already gone now. But we can talk about today. What we can see today we can prove. So how does the science of today support the ideology that is found in LGBTQ?", "I don't think that is accurate, by the way. I think the most studies today show that among the people who transition there's a highest rate of committing suicides, because especially a lot of these people who do these operations in the US, they do in a very young age, and then they're not able yet to make a big decision like that in their life. So when they grow older, it's not a reversible thing in most cases; they end up regretting it, and a lot of them end up committing suicide. A very clear example is the [Reimer] twins: in the end they both essentially took their own life. They would change without their View, and they were young.", "So I would say science is opposite, even the modern science. I'm not talking about back then. Even modern science, even though the societal view has changed, modern science is still against the fact that this is something that is genetic or born with.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Ex-Muslim Transgender Confronts Muslim On LGTBQ! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eGnyoF1UOh0", ((633, "10:33–11:01"), (674, "11:14–12:02"), (792, "13:12–13:28"),))
// U ML130:28-137 | The Muslim Lantern | 6
#passage(("A lot of people say, \"oh, that study was retracted!\" If this is what you're about to say, it's not really an argument, because it's not about him retracting it or not retracting it. He used a scientific methodology. He spoke to those people; he didn't force any of them.", "He's been criticized because America, after the sexual revolution and all of that, it has become the forbidden sin to criticize the LGBT community, and in certain cases, people can go to jail, not only in America, but in places in the West. Now, when that societal shift happened, science IS impacted. The studies are not as objective as you think they are. A lot of them are impacted by the social norms and social tendencies, and what people see as taboo, and what people don't see as taboo.", "So in this case, he was first criticized for the word \"Reparative\". So you see, it was a gradual thing until he retracted the study, or they took it off from Nature! It was on Nature Magazine. They took it off. It's still there! You can find it in PubMed and all of these different websites which show you scientific studies. Then he changed the wording. Then he was criticized again until he had to remove it. And there's all of these interviews that happened to him from the press where they're all attacking him. And this is not just a \"nobody\"! That's why there was so much pressure put on him. He was called the father of Modern Psychology in America at the time. He's a very, very influential figure.", "And that's why it's very, very difficult for anyone to conduct similar studies, like the one of Robert Spitzer, due to the fact that they don't allow them to do these studies. The universities are not going to accept it. They're not going to peer review it. I have many friends, by the way, who tried. But they're not allowed. They don't peer review them. They reject them! The professors are supposed to peer review. The people who need to look at it, they don't! They look at these things as taboo. They don't want to go into this: \"I'm going to lose my job\", \"I'm going to be cancelled\". That's the word that some people use. Some people like their jobs, and they just don't want to go into that.", "And a lot of people do similar things with Evolution, or the existence of God. There is a documentary called \"The Expelled\", and within that documentary, you can see people, for example, who mentioned God's existence in their research, how they're excommunicated, or that talk against Evolution. So, the societal acceptance of certain things, the societal norms and societal worldviews impact studies. But in the end, you cannot present me a scientific fault in his study. You can't! Because the study was valid.", "He brought 200 people. They were all homosexuals. I believe it was a period of five years or something like that. Don't quote me exactly on how many years now. We can speak to each other for 10,000 years, not five years: I'm NEVER going to start telling you \"I'm attracted to men now\". It's not going to happen, because I'm born, and I'm naturally just how a human body, male and female, is created. I don't want to get graphic here. But you have two body parts that perfectly fit with each other. You have a process of reproduction, which is essential to the existence of Human race. You have these two body parts releasing certain lubricants, etc., because they're naturally designed for that specific action to take place. So I can present many things. This is how God has made us, has created us.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Tran$gender Challenges Muslim On His Comments About LGTBQ Community! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XPGGQKYFYqo", ((129, "2:09–2:21"), (151, "2:31–3:00"), (185, "3:05–3:54"), (236, "3:56–4:28"), (272, "4:32–5:00"), (308, "5:08–6:35"),))
